2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://gw.geneanet.org/foullon?lang=en&pz=matheline+marie+herminia&nz=foullon&p=elzbieta+piast+ou+elisabeth+von+polen+kalisch+kalisch+kalisz&n=de+pologne
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Elżbieta''' – '''księżniczka wielkopolska''', '''księżna legnicko-wrocławska''' z dynastii Piastów. http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/El%C5%BCbieta_Boles%C5%82aw%C3%B3wna
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Greater Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Greater Poland (1263- 28 September 1304) was the eldest child of Boleslaw the Pious and his wife Jolenta of Poland. Her younger sister was Jadwiga of Greater Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1273, Elisabeth married Henry V the Fat, Duke of Legnica (1248-1296) who was the son of Bolesław II the Bald and Hedwig of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig (b. ca. 1277 - d. aft. 3 February 1347), married by 1289/95 to Prince Otto of Brandenburg-Salzwedel, second son of Margrave Otto V. After her husband died, she became a nun in St. Klara, Wroclaw.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphemia (b. ca. 1278 - d. June 1347), married in 1300 to Otto II of Görz, Duke of Carinthia. Through this union, Elisabeth and Henry were a direct ancestor of the Kings of Sicily, Aragon and Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna (b. 1284 - d. 2/3 October 1343), Abbess of St. Klara, Wroclaw.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (b. ca. 1290 - d. Nov 1357/58), Abbess of St. Klara, Wroclaw.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bolesław III the Generous (b. 23 September 1291 - d. 21 April 1352).
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena (b. ca. 1293 - d. aft. 1300), nun in St. Klara, Gniezno.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry VI the Good (b. 18 March 1294 - d. 24 November 1335).
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław (b. posthumously, 6 June 1296 - d. 13 January aft. 1352).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband went to war with Henry III, Duke of Silesia-Glogau, he was captured and imprisoned in Glogau until he was released. Henry was ill by the time of his release; this was due to the conditions of his confinement (he was lo
2 CONC cked in an iron cage).
2 CONT
2 CONT He never recovered his health, and seems to have known that he was not going to live much longer. Fearing for the future of his Duchy, in 1294 he decided to put himself under the protection of the Holy See.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry died on 22 February 1296 and was buried in the Clare monastery of Wroclaw. At this time, all his sons were minors, and because of this, they were put under the tutelage of his brother Bolko.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth's mother and younger sister, Anna had gone to live in a nunnery after the death of Anna, Jadwiga and Elisabeth's father. Jadwiga was married to Władysław I the Elbow-high, they had many children; among them were Elizabet
2 CONC h of Poland and Casimir III of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth and her children went to live with Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and his family. Here, Wenceslaus and Elisabeth arranged for her son Bolesław to marry Wenceslaus' youngest daughter Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since Bolesław's arrival to the Bohemian court and after his betrothal to Margaret, the King clearly favored him; this attitude caused fear between the closest male relatives of the King, who seen the young Duke of Legnica in a po
2 CONC tential rival for the throne. Although Wenceslaus II had a son seemed him irrelevant, the sudden death of the King in 1305 and one year later the murdered of his son and successor Wenceslaus III in Olomouc gained him an unexpected
2 CONC ly importance. Bolesław began his fight for the Bohemian throne taking the title of "haeres Regni Poloniae" (heir of the Polish Kingdom).
2 CONT
2 CONT Bolesław and Margaret were married in 1308 and had three children. By this time, Elisabeth had died. She died on 28 September 1304 and was buried alongside her husband in the Clare monastery of Wroclaw.
