2 CONT
2 CONT SDHK-nr: [http://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=vadstena&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&FacettFilter=register_facet%24Brev%3a%7cregister_facet%24Brev%2fSDHK+(medeltidsbrev)%3a&FacettState=xXo49g%3ao%7cundefined%3ac%7cAXR
2 CONC OIQ%3ac%7c&page=10&FacettLimits=xXo49g%3a0&postid=sdhk_5459&tab=post#tab 5459]
2 CONT
2 CONT Utfärdat: 13470715, Lödöse Innehåll: Kung Magnus och drottning Blanka upprättar sitt norska testamente, vari de till klostret i Vadstena, där de väljer sin grav, skänker 6 000 mark penningar, och som säkerhet lämnar Orust i pant. 
2 CONC (Se vid...
2 CONT
2 CONT ===In English===:
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus II Eriksson or Magnus VII of Norway and Magnus IV (II) of Sweden was king of Sweden (spring 1316 – December 1, 1374), Norway, and Terra Scania, and was son of Duke Erik Magnusson of Sweden and Ingeborg, daughter of Haakon V
2 CONC  of Norway. Also known by his nickname "Magnus Smek" (Eng. "Pet-Magnus").
2 CONT
2 CONT Referring to Magnus Eriksson as Magnus IV is a later invention. The Swedish kings Erik XIV (1560-68) and Charles IX (1604-1611) took their numbers after studying a highly fictitious History of Sweden.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was elected king of Sweden on 8 July 1319, and acclaimed as hereditary king of Norway at the thing of Haugathing in Tønsberg in August the same year. Under the Regencies of his Grandmother Queen Helvig and his Mother Duches
2 CONC s Ingeborg the countries were ruled by Knut Jonsson and Erling Vidkunsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was declared to have come of age at 15 in 1331. This caused resistance in Norway, where a statute from 1302 made clear that kings came of age at the age of 20, and a rising by Erling Vidkunsson and other Norwegian nobles en
2 CONC sued. In 1333, the rebels submitted to king Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1332 the king of Denmark, Christopher II, died as a "king without a country" after he and his older brother and predecessor had pawned Denmark piece by piece. King Magnus took advantage of his neighbour's distress, redeeming th
2 CONC e pawn for the eastern Danish provinces for a huge amount of silver, and thus became ruler also of Terra Scania.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 July 1336 Magnus was crowned king of both Norway and Sweden in Stockholm. This caused further resentment in Norway, where the nobles and magnates wished a separate Norwegian coronation. A second rising by members of the high
2 CONC  nobility of Norway ensued in 1338.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of his many formal expansions his rule was considered a period of decrease both to the Swedish royal power and to Sweden as a whole. Foreign nations like Denmark (after its recovery in 1340) and Mecklenburg intervened and
2 CONC  Magnus himself does not seem to have been able to resist the internal opposition. He was regarded a weak king and criticised because for giving favourites too much power.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1336 he married Blanche of Namur, daughter of Count Jean of Namur and Marie of Artois, a descendant of Louis VIII of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Opposition to Magnus' rule in Norway led to a settlement between the king and the Norwegian nobility at Varberg on 15 August 1343. In violation of the Norwegian laws on royal inheritance, Magnus' younger son Håkon would become kin
2 CONC g of Norway, with Magnus as regent during his minority. Later the same year, it was declared that Magnus' older son, Eric would become king of Sweden on Magnus' death. Thus, the union between Norway and Sweden would be severed. Th
2 CONC is occurred when Håkon came of age in 1355.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus' young favorite courtier was Bengt Algotsson, whom he elevated to Duke of Finland and Halland, as well as Viceroy of the province of Scania. Because homosexuality was a mortal sin and vehemently scorned at that time, revela
2 CONC tions about the king's alleged love relationship with Algotsson, and other erotic excapades, were spread by his enemies, particularly by some noblemen who referred to mystical visions of St. Bridget (Birgitta) [2]. The allegations
2 CONC  earned Magnus the epithet of Magnus the Petter (M. Smek), and caused him a lot of harm, but there is no factual basis for them in historical sources. Magnus and Blanche had at least five children, of whom three daughters died in 
2 CONC infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of the raise in taxation to pay for the acquisition of the Scanian province, some Swedish nobles supported by the Church attempted to oust Magnus, setting up his elder son Eric as king (Eric XII of Sweden), but Eric died s
2 CONC upposedly of the plague in 1359, with his wife Beatrice of Brandenburg and their two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Valdemar IV of Denmark conquered Terra Scania in 1360. He went on to conquer Gotland in 1361. On the 27th of July, 1361, outside the city of Visby, the main city of Gotland, the final battle took place. It ended in a complete
2 CONC  victory for Valdemar. Magnus had warned the inhabitants of Visby in a letter and started to gather troops to reconquer Scania. Valdemar went home to Denmark again in August and took a lot of plunder with him. Either in late 1361 
2 CONC or early 1362 the inhabitants of Visby raised themselves against the few Danish that Valdemar left behind and killed them. In 1363 a rebellion against Magnus broke out. It was supported by Valdemar and resulted a few months later 
2 CONC (February 1364) in that Magnus was deposed from the Swedish throne being replaced by the Duke of Mecklenburg's son Albert of Sweden. Magnus was seeking refuge with his younger son in Norway, where he drowned in 1374.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to an allegedly autobiographic account known as the "Rukopisanie Magnusha" (Magnus's Testament) which has been inserted into the Russian Sofia First Chronicle composed in Novgorod (against whom Magnus had crusaded in the
2 CONC  1340s and 50s), Magnus in fact, did not drown at sea, but saw the errors of his ways and converted to Orthodoxy, becoming a monk in a Novgorodian monastery in Karelia. The account is apocryphal
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus IV of Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign	8 July 1319 – February 1364
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation	21 July 1336, Stockholm
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor Birger Magnusson
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor	Albert
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign	August 1319 – 1343
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation	21 July 1336, Stockholm
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor	Haakon V
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor	Haakon VI
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse	Blanka of Namur
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric XII of Sweden (1339–1359)
2 CONT
2 CONT Haakon VI of Norway (1340–1380)
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Erik Magnusson (c. 1282–1318)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Ingeborg Håkonsdotter (1301–1361)
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	Spring 1316
2 CONT
2 CONT Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Died	December 1, 1374 (Aged 58)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bømlofjord, Norway (shipwreck)
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus IV of Sweden (Swedish: Magnus Eriksson; spring 1316 – 1 December 1374), also Magnus VII of Norway, was king of Sweden, Norway, and Scania. He has also vindictively been called Magnus Smek (English equivalent: Magnus the Car
2 CONC esser). Referring to Magnus Eriksson as Magnus II is inaccurate. The Swedish Royal Court officially lists three Swedish kings before him by the name.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was the son of Duke Erik Magnusson of Sweden and Ingeborg, a daughter of Haakon V of Norway. Magnus was elected king of Sweden on 8 July 1319, and acclaimed as hereditary king of Norway at the thing of Haugathing in Tønsber
2 CONC g in August the same year. Under the Regencies of his Grandmother Queen Helvig and his Mother Duchess Ingeborg the countries were ruled by Knut Jonsson and Erling Vidkunsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was declared to have come of age at 15 in 1331. This caused resistance in Norway, where a statute from 1302 made clear that kings came of age at the age of 20, and a rising by Erling Vidkunsson and other Norwegian nobles en
2 CONC sued. In 1333, the rebels submitted to king Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1332 the king of Denmark, Christopher II, died as a "king without a country" after he and his older brother and predecessor had pawned Denmark piece by piece. King Magnus took advantage of his neighbour's distress, redeeming th
2 CONC e pawn for the eastern Danish provinces for a huge amount of silver, and thus became ruler also of Skåneland.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 July 1336 Magnus was crowned king of both Norway and Sweden in Stockholm. This caused further resentment in Norway, where the nobles and magnates wished a separate Norwegian coronation. A second rising by members of the high
2 CONC  nobility of Norway ensued in 1338.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1336 he married Blanche of Namur, daughter of Count Jean of Namur and Marie of Artois, a descendant of Louis VIII of France. The wedding took place in October or early November 1335, possibly at Bohus castle. As a wedding gift 
2 CONC Blanche received the province of Tunsberg in Norway and Lödöse in Sweden as fiefs. Together they had two sons, Eric and Haakon. Magnus and Blanche had at least five children, of whom three daughters died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Opposition to Magnus' rule in Norway led to a settlement between the king and the Norwegian nobility at Varberg on 15 August 1343. In violation of the Norwegian laws on royal inheritance, Magnus' younger son Haakon would become ki
2 CONC ng of Norway, with Magnus as regent during his minority. Later the same year, it was declared that Magnus' older son, Eric would become king of Sweden on Magnus' death. Thus, the union between Norway and Sweden would be severed. T
2 CONC his occurred when Haakon came of age in 1355.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of the raise in taxation to pay for the acquisition of the Scanian province, some Swedish nobles supported by the Church attempted to oust Magnus, setting up his elder son Erik Magnusson as king (Eric XII of Sweden), but E
2 CONC ric died supposedly of the plague in 1359, with his wife Beatrix of Bavaria, daughter of Louis IV of Bavaria and their two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==The Peace of Nöteborg==
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 August 1323, Magnus concluded the first treaty between Sweden and Novgorod (represented by Grand Prince Yury of Moscow) at Nöteborg (Orekhov) where Lake Ladoga empties into the Neva River.[3] The treaty delineated spheres of
2 CONC  influence among the Finns and Karelians and was supposed to be an "eternal peace", but Magnus' relations with Russia were not so peaceful. In 1337, religious strife between Orthodox Karelians and the Swedes led to a Swedish attac
2 CONC k on the town of Korela (Keksholm, Priozersk) and Vyborg (Vipuri), in which the Novgorodian and Ladogan merchants there were slaughtered. A Swedish commander named Sten also captured the fortress at Orekhov. Negotiations with the 
2 CONC Novgorodian mayor (Posadnik) Fedor were inconclusive and the Swedes attacked Karelians around Lake Ladoga and Lake Onega before a peace was concluded in 1339 along the old terms of the 1323 treaty. In this treaty, the Swedes claim
2 CONC ed that Sten and others acted on their own without the consent of the king.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Crusade against Novgorod==
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations were quiet between Sweden and Novgorod until 1348, when Magnus led a crusade against Novgorod, marching up the Neva, forcibly converting the tribes along that river, and briefly capturing the fortress of Orekhov for a se
2 CONC cond time.[5] The Novgorodians retook the fortress in 1349 after a seven month siege, and Magnus fell back, in large part due to the ravages of the plague farther West. While he spent much of 1351 trying to drum up support for fur
2 CONC ther crusading action among the German cities in the Baltic States, he never returned to attack Novgorod.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Later years==
2 CONT
2 CONT King Valdemar IV of Denmark conquered Scania in 1360. He went on to conquer Gotland in 1361. On the 27th of July, 1361, outside the city of Visby, the main city of Gotland, the final battle took place. It ended in a complete victo
2 CONC ry for Valdemar. Magnus had warned the inhabitants of Visby in a letter and started to gather troops to reconquer Scania. Valdemar went home to Denmark again in August and took a lot of plunder with him. Either in late 1361 or ear
2 CONC ly 1362 the inhabitants of Visby raised themselves against the few Danish that Valdemar left behind and killed them.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1363, members of the Swedish Council of Aristocracy, led by Bo Jonsson Grip, arrived in the court of Mecklenburg. They had been banished from the country after a revolt against king Magnus Eriksson. At the nobles' request, Albr
2 CONC ekt of Mecklenburg launched an invasion of Sweden supported by several German dukes and counts. Several Hanseatic cities and dukes in Northern Germany expressed support of the new king. Stockholm and Kalmar, with large Hanseatic p
2 CONC opulations, and also welcomed the intervention. Albrekt was proclaimed King of Sweden and officially crowned on 18 February 1364. Magnus was seeking refuge with his younger son in Norway, where he drowned in 1374.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Evaluation of his reign==
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of his many formal expansions his rule was considered a period of decrease both to the Swedish royal power and to Sweden as a whole. Foreign nations like Denmark (after its recovery in 1340) and Mecklenburg intervened and
2 CONC  Magnus himself does not seem to have been able to resist the internal opposition. He was regarded a weak king and criticised because for giving favourites too much power.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus' young favourite courtier was Bengt Algotsson, whom he elevated to Duke of Finland and Halland, as well as Viceroy of the province of Scania. Because homosexuality was a mortal sin and vehemently scorned at that time, rumou
2 CONC rs about the king's alleged love relationship with Algotsson, and other erotic excapades, were spread by his enemies, particularly by some noblemen who referred to mystical visions of St. Bridget (Birgitta). The allegations earned
2 CONC  Magnus the epithet of Magnus the Caresser and caused him a lot of harm, but there is no factual basis for them in historical sources.[7].
2 CONT
2 CONT In a bit of propagandist retaliations against Magnus, the Russians drew up an allegedly autobiographic account known as the Testament of Magnus (Rukopisanie Magnusha) which has been inserted into the Russian Sofia First Chronicle,
2 CONC  composed in Novgorod, which claimed that Magnus in fact, did not drown at sea, but saw the errors of his ways and converted to Orthodoxy, becoming a monk in a Novgorodian monastery in Karelia. The account is apocryphal.[8]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Magnus II Eriksson or Magnus VII of Norway and Magnus IV (II) of Sweden was king of Sweden (spring 1316 – December 1, 1374), Norway, and Terra Scania, and was son of Duke Erik Magnusson of Sweden and Ingeborg, daughter of Haakon V
2 CONC  of Norway. Also known by his nickname "Magnus Smek" (Eng. "Pet-Magnus").
2 CONT
2 CONT Referring to Magnus Eriksson as Magnus IV is a later invention. The Swedish kings Erik XIV (1560-68) and Charles IX (1604-1611) took their numbers after studying a highly fictitious History of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was elected king of Sweden on 8 July 1319, and acclaimed as hereditary king of Norway at the thing of Haugathing in Tønsberg in August the same year. Under the Regencies of his Grandmother Queen Helvig and his Mother Duches
2 CONC s Ingeborg the countries were ruled by Knut Jonsson and Erling Vidkunsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was declared to have come of age at 15 in 1331. This caused resistance in Norway, where a statute from 1302 made clear that kings came of age at the age of 20, and a rising by Erling Vidkunsson and other Norwegian nobles en
2 CONC sued. In 1333, the rebels submitted to king Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1332 the king of Denmark, Christopher II, died as a "king without a country" after he and his older brother and predecessor had pawned Denmark piece by piece. King Magnus took advantage of his neighbour's distress, redeeming th
2 CONC e pawn for the eastern Danish provinces for a huge amount of silver, and thus became ruler also of Terra Scania.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 July 1336 Magnus was crowned king of both Norway and Sweden in Stockholm. This caused further resentment in Norway, where the nobles and magnates wished a separate Norwegian coronation. A second rising by members of the high
2 CONC  nobility of Norway ensued in 1338.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of his many formal expansions his rule was considered a period of decrease both to the Swedish royal power and to Sweden as a whole. Foreign nations like Denmark (after its recovery in 1340) and Mecklenburg intervened and
2 CONC  Magnus himself does not seem to have been able to resist the internal opposition. He was regarded a weak king and criticised because for giving favourites too much power.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1336 he married Blanche of Namur, daughter of Count Jean of Namur and Marie of Artois, a descendant of Louis VIII of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Opposition to Magnus' rule in Norway led to a settlement between the king and the Norwegian nobility at Varberg on 15 August 1343. In violation of the Norwegian laws on royal inheritance, Magnus' younger son Håkon would become kin
2 CONC g of Norway, with Magnus as regent during his minority. Later the same year, it was declared that Magnus' older son, Eric would become king of Sweden on Magnus' death. Thus, the union between Norway and Sweden would be severed. Th
2 CONC is occurred when Håkon came of age in 1355.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus' young favourite courtier was Bengt Algotsson, whom he elevated to Duke of Finland and Halland, as well as Viceroy of the province of Scania. Because homosexuality was a mortal sin and vehemently scorned at that time, revel
2 CONC ations about the king's alleged love relationship with Algotsson, and other erotic excapades, were spread by his enemies, particularly by some noblemen who referred to mystical visions of St. Bridget (Birgitta) . The allegations e
2 CONC arned Magnus the epithet of Magnus the Petter (M. Smek), and caused him a lot of harm, but there is no factual basis for them in historical sources. Magnus and Blanche had at least five children, of whom three daughters died in in
2 CONC fancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of the raise in taxation to pay for the acquisition of the Scanian province, some Swedish nobles supported by the Church attempted to oust Magnus, setting up his elder son Eric as king (Eric XII of Sweden), but Eric died s
2 CONC upposedly of the plague in 1359, with his wife Beatrice of Brandenburg and their two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Valdemar IV of Denmark conquered Terra Scania in 1360. He went on to conquer Gotland in 1361. On the 27th of July, 1361, outside the city of Visby, the main city of Gotland, the final battle took place. It ended in a complete
2 CONC  victory for Valdemar. Magnus had warned the inhabitants of Visby in a letter and started to gather troops to reconquer Scania. Valdemar went home to Denmark again in August and took a lot of plunder with him. Either in late 1361 
2 CONC or early 1362 the inhabitants of Visby raised themselves against the few Danish that Valdemar left behind and killed them. In 1363 a rebellion against Magnus broke out. It was supported by Valdemar and resulted a few months later 
2 CONC (February 1364) in that Magnus was deposed from the Swedish throne being replaced by the Duke of Mecklenburg's son Albert of Sweden. Magnus was seeking refuge with his younger son in Norway, where he drowned in 1374.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to an allegedly autobiographic account known as the "Rukopisanie Magnusha" (Magnus's Testament) which has been inserted into the Russian Sofia First Chronicle composed in Novgorod (against whom Magnus had crusaded in the
2 CONC  1340s and 50s), Magnus in fact, did not drown at sea, but saw the errors of his ways and converted to Orthodoxy, becoming a monk in a Novgorodian monastery in Karelia. The account is apocryphal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Källor==
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Diplomatarium Norvegicum (volumes I-XXI). Officiellt påbud från kung Magnus Eriksson i Helsingborg, daterat 4 juli 1343. Document Nr. 220, Riksarkivet. Utlagt av Dokumentasjonsprosjektet (Universitetet i Bergen, Universite
2 CONC tet i Oslo, Universitetet i Trondheim og Universitetet i Tromsø): Magnus medr guds nad Noreghs Svyia ok Skana konongr (Magnus med Guds Nåd Norges Sveriges och Skånes konung).
2 CONT    2. ^ Tegnér,Göran och Nina Folin. Medeltidens ABC. Statens historiska museum, 1985. ISBN 91-7844-041-6. s. 314.
2 CONT    3. ^ Henrikson, Alf och Björn Berg. Dansk historia. Stockholm: Bonnier, 1989. ISBN 91-0-047565-3. s. 170.
2 CONT    4. ^ Lars O. Lagerqvist & Nils Åberg Litet lexikon över Sveriges regenter Vincent förlag, Boda kyrkby 2004 ISBN: 91-87064-43-X s. 19
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Jerker Rosén: Gunvor Grenholm: Den svenska historien. Band 2. Medeltid 1319-1520, Albert Bonniers Förlag, Stockholm 1966, sid. 14-47. 
2 CONT     * Magnus, 4. Magnus Eriksson i Nordisk familjebok (2:a upplagan, 1912)
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnus_IV_of_Sweden
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnus_VII_of_Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnus_IV_of_Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus IV of Sweden (Swedish: Magnus Eriksson; spring 1316 – 1 December 1374), also Magnus VII of Norway, was king of Sweden, Norway, and Scania. He has also vindictively been called Magnus Smek (English equivalent: Magnus the Car
2 CONC esser). Referring to Magnus Eriksson as Magnus II is inaccurate. The Swedish Royal Court officially lists three Swedish kings before him by the name.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was the son of Duke Erik Magnusson of Sweden and Ingeborg, a daughter of Haakon V of Norway. Magnus was elected king of Sweden on 8 July 1319, and acclaimed as hereditary king of Norway at the thing of Haugathing in Tønsber
2 CONC g in August the same year. Under the Regencies of his Grandmother Queen Helvig and his Mother Duchess Ingeborg the countries were ruled by Knut Jonsson and Erling Vidkunsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was declared to have come of age at 15 in 1331. This caused resistance in Norway, where a statute from 1302 made clear that kings came of age at the age of 20, and a rising by Erling Vidkunsson and other Norwegian nobles en
2 CONC sued. In 1333, the rebels submitted to king Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1332 the king of Denmark, Christopher II, died as a "king without a country" after he and his older brother and predecessor had pawned Denmark piece by piece. King Magnus took advantage of his neighbour's distress, redeeming th
2 CONC e pawn for the eastern Danish provinces for a huge amount of silver, and thus became ruler also of Skåneland.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 July 1336 Magnus was crowned king of both Norway and Sweden in Stockholm. This caused further resentment in Norway, where the nobles and magnates wished a separate Norwegian coronation. A second rising by members of the high
2 CONC  nobility of Norway ensued in 1338.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1336 he married Blanche of Namur, daughter of Count Jean of Namur and Marie of Artois, a descendant of Louis VIII of France. The wedding took place in October or early November 1335, possibly at Bohus castle. As a wedding gift 
2 CONC Blanche received the province of Tunsberg in Norway and Lödöse in Sweden as fiefs. Together they had two sons, Eric and Haakon. Magnus and Blanche had at least five children, of whom three daughters died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Opposition to Magnus' rule in Norway led to a settlement between the king and the Norwegian nobility at Varberg on 15 August 1343. In violation of the Norwegian laws on royal inheritance, Magnus' younger son Haakon would become ki
2 CONC ng of Norway, with Magnus as regent during his minority. Later the same year, it was declared that Magnus' older son, Eric would become king of Sweden on Magnus' death. Thus, the union between Norway and Sweden would be severed. T
2 CONC his occurred when Haakon came of age in 1355.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of the raise in taxation to pay for the acquisition of the Scanian province, some Swedish nobles supported by the Church attempted to oust Magnus, setting up his elder son Erik Magnusson as king (Eric XII of Sweden), but E
2 CONC ric died supposedly of the plague in 1359, with his wife Beatrix of Bavaria, daughter of Louis IV of Bavaria and their two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Peace of Nöteborg
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 August 1323, Magnus concluded the first treaty between Sweden and Novgorod (represented by Grand Prince Yury of Moscow) at Nöteborg (Orekhov) where Lake Ladoga empties into the Neva River.[3] The treaty delineated spheres of
2 CONC  influence among the Finns and Karelians and was supposed to be an "eternal peace", but Magnus' relations with Russia were not so peaceful. In 1337, religious strife between Orthodox Karelians and the Swedes led to a Swedish attac
2 CONC k on the town of Korela (Keksholm, Priozersk) and Vyborg (Vipuri), in which the Novgorodian and Ladogan merchants there were slaughtered. A Swedish commander named Sten also captured the fortress at Orekhov. Negotiations with the 
2 CONC Novgorodian mayor (Posadnik) Fedor were inconclusive and the Swedes attacked Karelians around Lake Ladoga and Lake Onega before a peace was concluded in 1339 along the old terms of the 1323 treaty. In this treaty, the Swedes claim
2 CONC ed that Sten and others acted on their own without the consent of the king.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Crusade against Novgorod
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations were quiet between Sweden and Novgorod until 1348, when Magnus led a crusade against Novgorod, marching up the Neva, forcibly converting the tribes along that river, and briefly capturing the fortress of Orekhov for a se
2 CONC cond time.[5] The Novgorodians retook the fortress in 1349 after a seven month siege, and Magnus fell back, in large part due to the ravages of the plague farther West. While he spent much of 1351 trying to drum up support for fur
2 CONC ther crusading action among the German cities in the Baltic States, he never returned to attack Novgorod.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT King Valdemar IV of Denmark conquered Scania in 1360. He went on to conquer Gotland in 1361. On the 27th of July, 1361, outside the city of Visby, the main city of Gotland, the final battle took place. It ended in a complete victo
2 CONC ry for Valdemar. Magnus had warned the inhabitants of Visby in a letter and started to gather troops to reconquer Scania. Valdemar went home to Denmark again in August and took a lot of plunder with him. Either in late 1361 or ear
2 CONC ly 1362 the inhabitants of Visby raised themselves against the few Danish that Valdemar left behind and killed them.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1363, members of the Swedish Council of Aristocracy, led by Bo Jonsson Grip, arrived in the court of Mecklenburg. They had been banished from the country after a revolt against king Magnus Eriksson. At the nobles' request, Albr
2 CONC ekt of Mecklenburg launched an invasion of Sweden supported by several German dukes and counts. Several Hanseatic cities and dukes in Northern Germany expressed support of the new king. Stockholm and Kalmar, with large Hanseatic p
2 CONC opulations, and also welcomed the intervention. Albrekt was proclaimed King of Sweden and officially crowned on 18 February 1364. Magnus was seeking refuge with his younger son in Norway, where he drowned in 1374.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evaluation of his reign
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of his many formal expansions his rule was considered a period of decrease both to the Swedish royal power and to Sweden as a whole. Foreign nations like Denmark (after its recovery in 1340) and Mecklenburg intervened and
2 CONC  Magnus himself does not seem to have been able to resist the internal opposition. He was regarded a weak king and criticised for giving favourites too much power.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus' young favourite courtier was Bengt Algotsson, whom he elevated to Duke of Finland and Halland, as well as Viceroy of the province of Scania. Because homosexuality was a mortal sin and vehemently scorned at that time, rumou
2 CONC rs about the king's alleged love relationship with Algotsson, and other erotic escapades, were spread by his enemies, particularly by some noblemen who referred to mystical visions of St. Bridget (Birgitta). The allegations earned
2 CONC  Magnus the epithet of Magnus the Caresser and caused him a lot of harm, but there is no factual basis for them in historical sources.[7].
2 CONT
2 CONT In a bit of propagandist retaliations against Magnus, the Russians drew up an allegedly autobiographic account known as the Testament of Magnus (Rukopisanie Magnusha) which has been inserted into the Russian Sofia First Chronicle,
2 CONC  composed in Novgorod, which claimed that Magnus in fact, did not drown at sea, but saw the errors of his ways and converted to Orthodoxy, becoming a monk in a Novgorodian monastery in Karelia. The account is apocryphal.[8]
