3 CONT Roman Emperors, Websters Biographical Dictiona
1 NOTE Wenceslaus (or Wenzel) of Bohemia, 2nd son of Charles IV of Luxembourg, became Emperor of Germany in 1378, succeeded his father. Wenceslaus ruled until 1400, and was succeeded by Rupert of the Palatine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslas III was eighteen when he acceded to the throne of Bohemia in 1378.  A tragic figure, he emerges from the chronicles a king of Caliban, half clownish, half vicious, a composite of halt-truths, a confirmed alcoholic. - irr
2 CONC itable, black-tempered, and indolent as a soverign, and stayed in Prague to the neglect of the rest of the Holy Roman Empire.,
1 NOTE Wenceslas (1361-1419), Holy Roman emperor and king of Germany (1378-1400) 
2 CONT and, as Wenceslas IV, king of Bohemia (1378-1419). His weak, ineffectual 
2 CONT reign was marked by religious disorder, civil strife, and near anarchy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The son and successor of Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV, Wenceslas made his 
2 CONT capital in Prague and largely ignored his German territories, which were 
2 CONT rent with wars between the towns and the nobles; the German princes 
2 CONT finally deposed him for drunkenness and incompetence in 1400, choosing 
2 CONT Rupert of the Palatinate (1352-1410) in his stead. In Bohemia Wenceslas 
2 CONT fared little better. Harried by relatives and rebellious nobles, he was 
2 CONT imprisoned in 1394 by his cousin Jobst (1351-1411), margrave of Moravia, 
2 CONT who compelled the king to name him regent in Bohemia. In 1402 he was again 
2 CONT imprisoned and temporarily deposed, this time by his younger brother 
2 CONT Sigismund. Wenceslas was an early supporter of the Czech religious 
2 CONT reformer John Huss (Jan Hus) but did nothing to save him from the stake in 
2 CONT 1415 and subsequently turned upon his followers.
