1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jaśko of Oleśnica (also known as Jan of Oleśnica or Jan z Oleśnicy) was a powerful 15th-century Polish magnate and diplomat who shared a close historical connection with the Moskarzewski family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Historical Role and Connections==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Political Successor: He was the son of Dobiesław of Oleśnica and the grandson of Dobiesław of Kurozwęki. His family, the Kurozwęcki clan, rose to prominence alongside Klemens Moskarzewski during the reign of King Jagiełło.
2 CONT * Royal Influence: Like Moskarzewski, Jaśko was a trusted advisor to King Władysław II Jagiełło. He served as the Castellan of Kraków and the Starosta of Sandomierz, two of the highest offices in the Polish Kingdom.
2 CONT * Military and Diplomacy: He played a critical role in the Battle of Grunwald (1410) and was a signatory of the Union of Horodło (1413), which further cemented the Polish-Lithuanian alliance.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Relation to Moskarzewski==
2 CONT
2 CONT While they were from different noble houses (Moskarzewski from the Pilawa coat of arms and Jaśko from the Dębno coat of arms), they were peers in the royal court. Jaśko's family eventually acquired the title "of Oleśnica" from the
2 CONC ir estate in the Sandomierz Voivodeship, not to be confused with the Duchy of Oleśnica in Silesia.
