2 NOTE As a young man he lived at the German Imperial Court. He was crowned king in Denmark 1449, in Norway 1450 and in Sweden 1457. As a result of the Swedish rebellion against the king in 1464, Sweden left the Kalmar Union, only to be 
3 CONC reconquered in 1497 by Christian's son King John (Hans). Christian I was a popular monarch in Denmark. He further strengthened his position by visiting Pope Sixtus IV in Rome in 1474. The Cardinals in Rome said about the king: "A 
3 CONC beautiful animal - pity though, that he is mute" - he did not speak the Latin language. With the Pope's blessing, Christian I founded the University of Copenhagen in 1479. In 1460 he had been elected Duke of Schleswig and Count of
3 CONC  Holstein, and on that occasion he promised that his countries should be forever united "op ewig ungedeelt". When in 1469 his daughter Margaret married the Scottish king, James III, he took the Isles of Orkney and Shetland from hi
3 CONC s Norwegian kingdom and gave them to Scotland as her dowry.
