2 CONT
2 CONT Thepeerage: http://thepeerage.com/p10496.htm#i104959
2 CONT
2 CONT Geneall: http://www.geneall.net/F/per_page.php?id=11760
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT English: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella,_Duchess_of_Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Francais: http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabelle_Ire_de_Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella, Duchess of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Isabella, duchess of Lorraine)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella (1400 – February 28, 1453) was Duchess of Lorraine, suo jure, from 25 January 1431 to her death in 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Charles II, Duke of Lorraine and Margaret of the Palatinate. Her paternal grandparents were John I, Duke of Lorraine and Sophie of Wurttemberg. Her maternal grandparents were Rupert of Germany and El
2 CONC isabeth of Nuremberg, daughter of Burgrave Frederick V of Nuremberg and Elisabeth of Meissen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella inherited the duchy from her father upon his death, and ruled jointly with her husband, Duke René of Anjou, also Duke of Bar and King of Naples, whom she had married in 1419. During his absence, she acted as regent for hi
2 CONC s domains. She led an army to rescue her husband from Philip III, Duke of Burgundy, who held René in captivity from 1434 until 1436.
2 CONT
2 CONT René and Isabella had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John II of Anjou and King of Naples (1425-16 December 1470), married Marie de Bourbon, daughter of Charles I, Duke of Bourbon, by whom he had issue. He also had several illegitimate children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yolande of Anjou (2 November 1428-23 March 1483), married Frederick of Lorraine, count of Vaudemont; mother of duke René II of Lorraine, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Anjou (23 March 1430- 25 August 1482), married King Henry VI of England, by whom she had a son, Edward of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT plus seven others who died in childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella died on 28 February 1453 at the age of about 53 years. Her son John succeeded her as Duke of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT René married secondly, on 10 September 1454, Jeanne de Laval.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella, Duchess of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella (1400 – February 28, 1453) was Duchess of Lorraine from 1431 to her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of Charles II, Duke of Lorraine and Margaret of the Palatinate. Her maternal grandparents were Rupert of Germany and Elisabeth of Nuremberg, daughter of Burgrave Frederick V of Nuremberg and Elisabeth of Meissen
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella inherited the duchy from her father and ruled with her husband, Duke René of Anjou, also Duke of Bar and King of Naples. During his absence, she acted as regent for his domains. She led an army to rescue her husband from 
2 CONC Philip III, Duke of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT John II of Anjou and King of Naples (1425-1470)
2 CONT
2 CONT Yolande of Anjou (1428-1483), married Frederick of Lorraine, count of Vaudemont; mother of duke René II of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Anjou (1429-1482), married king Henry VI of England
2 CONT
2 CONT plus seven others who died in childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Salmonson, Jessica Amanda (1991) The Encyclopedia of Amazons. Paragon House. Page 126-7. ISBN 1-55778-420-5
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella (1400 – 28 February 1453) was suo jure Duchess of Lorraine, from 25 January 1431 to her death in 1453. She was queen consort of Naples by marriage to Duke René of Anjou, King of Naples, and the mother of Margaret of Anjou
2 CONC , Queen consort of England. Isabella served as general governor of her spouse's domains in France during his imprisonment in Burgundy, as well as regent in the Kingdom of Naples, in 1435-1438.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life Isabella was the eldest daughter of Charles II, Duke of Lorraine and Margaret of the Palatinate. By the death of her brothers, it was made apparent in 1410 that she would be the successor of her father in Lorraine. She was gi
2 CONC ven a careful education, and described as beautiful, witty, brave and with the ability to be careful and make hard decisions in difficult circumstances.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24 October 1420, she married René of Anjou. In the marriage contract, it was specified that she would inherit Lorraine, as he would inherit Bar and Pont-à-Mousson, and that their child and heir would inherit all their domains, 
2 CONC thereby uniting them.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 25 January 1431, Isabella inherited the duchy from her father upon his death, and ruled jointly with her husband as her co-ruler, as was customary for a female monarch at that time. Her right to rule was questioned by her cousi
2 CONC n, count Antoine de Vaudémont, who captured Rene in the Battle of Bulgnéville and had him imprisoned with his ally, the Duke of Burgundy. She led an army to rescue her husband from Philip III, Duke of Burgundy. She managed to secu
2 CONC re a ceasefire, and the Emperor recognized her right to rule 24 April 1434.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 1434, her imprisoned spouse inherited the domains of Anjou, Provence and Maine from his brother as well as the position of heir to the throne of Naples, and on 2 February 1435, her inherited the throne of Naples. Rene 
2 CONC appointed her to act as his general governor until his release, and a Napolese embassy asked her to come to Naples to assume the post of regent until her spouse could do so in person. She left with her son Louis and a fleet. The m
2 CONC id-16th century Chronicle of Gaspare Fuscolillo records that Isabella arrived in Naples on 15 October 1435. As regent of Naples, was to face the struggle with the other competitor to the throne of Naples, Alfonso of Aragon. She wa
2 CONC s given military support from the pope, but could not cooperate well with its commander, Jacopo Caldora.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rene was released in 1437, and arrived in Naples to take over the rule from Isabella in May 1438. Isabella left with Louis to return to Lorraine in August 1440. In Lorraine, she finally defeated Antoine de Vaudémont 27 March 1441.
2 CONC  When Charles VII of France visited in Nancy, they introduced Agnes Sorel to him, who was one of Isabella's ladies-in-waiting. She soon afterwards became the king's influential mistress. In July 1445, Isabella appointed her son Jo
2 CONC hn to be her governor general in Lorraine, and retired to her manor Launay in Saumur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella died on 28 February 1453 at the age of 53. Her son John succeeded her as Duke of Lorraine. She was buried in Angers Cathedral. René then married, on 10 September 1454, Jeanne de Laval, but this marriage was childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue René and Isabella had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John II (2 August 1424 – 16 December 1470), Duke of Lorraine and King of Naples, married Marie de Bourbon, daughter of Charles I, Duke of Bourbon, by whom he had issue. He also had several illegitimate children. Louis (16 October 
2 CONC 1427 – between 22 May and 16 October 1444), Marquis of Pont-à-Mousson and Lieutenant General of Lorraine. At the age of five, in 1432, he was sent as a hostage to Dijon with his brother John in exchange for their captive father. J
2 CONC ohn was released, but Louis was not and died of pneumonia in prison. Nicholas (2 November 1428 – 1430), twin with Yolande. Yolande (2 November 1428 – 23 March 1483), married Frederick of Lorraine, count of Vaudemont; mother, among
2 CONC  others, of duke René II of Lorraine. Margaret (23 March 1430 – 25 August 1482), married King Henry VI of England, by whom she had a son, Edward of Westminster, Prince of Wales. Charles (1431 – 1432), Count of Guise. Isabelle (die
2 CONC d young). René (died young). Louise (1436 – 1438). Anne (1437 – 1450, buried in Gardanne).
