1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katharina_van_Kleef https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_Cleves_(1417–1479) -- English version
2 CONT
2 CONT Katharina van Kleef ( May 25, 1417 - Lobith , February 20, 1476 ) was the wife of Arnold van Egmont , duke of Gelre . She was a daughter of Adolf IV van Kleef-Mark and Maria van Bourgondië . [1] She was a cousin of Philip the Good
2 CONC  .
2 CONT
2 CONT Life  Katharina van Kleef weapon.svg
2 CONT
2 CONT Katharina married Arnold at the age of thirteen. In the power struggle that broke out between her husband and her son, Adolf van Egmont , Katharina chose her son's side. With the knowledge of Katharina, her son was detained by her
2 CONC  son. [2] Katharina and her husband did not live in a fixed place. Favorite places were Valkhof in Nijmegen , Rosendael Castle in Rozendaal and Lobith Castle . [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:  Katharina and Arnold had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria ( Grave , 1431 - Roxburgh Castle , Edinburgh 1463), married to James II of Scotland [3] [4] Willem (* 1434, † young) [3] Margaretha (1436 - Simmern , 2 November 1486), married on 16 August 1454 in Lobith to Frederick I of Pa
2 CONC latinate-Simmern (24 April 1417 - Simmern , 2 November 1480) [3] [5] Adolf ( Grave , 1439 - Tournai , 1477), Duke of Gelre 1465–1471 and 1473–1477 [6] Catharina van Gelre (1439–1497), regent of Gelre 1477-1481; presumably married 
2 CONC Louis of Bourbon (c. 1436 - Wez (near Liège ) 30 August 1482), prince-bishop of Liège [3] [7] Book of Hours  From October 10, 2009 to January 3, 2010, there was an exhibition in Museum Het Valkhof in Nijmegen about the Book of Hou
2 CONC rs by Katharina van Kleef and 'the world of Catherine'. [2] [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hours_of_Catherine_of_Cleves '''Catherine of Cleves''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine (25 May 1417 – 10 February 1479) was Duchess of Guelders. The Hours of Catherine of Cleves was commissioned for her.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Family
2 CONT * 2 Book of Hours
2 CONT * 3 Life
2 CONT * 4 Issue
2 CONT * 5 Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family=
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine was the daughter of Adolph I, Duke of Cleves and Marie of Burgundy. She was a niece of Philip the Good.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Book of Hours=
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hours of Catherine of Cleves was commissioned for her when she married Arnold, Duke of Guelders, on 26 January 1430. It shows her lineage, as well as herself in prayer. The hours had been lost for four hundred years before res
2 CONC urfacing in 1856. It is one of the most richly decorated books of its kind that is preserved.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Life=
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine lived with her parents until 1431, despite already having been married the year before. She had close ties with Philip of Burgundy, who was mistrusted by her husband. Catherine had her daughter Mary raised at the Burgund
2 CONC ian court. When her husband punished Driel, he lost support in his duchy. Catherine acted as intermediate between her husband and the Estates of the realm. In 1450, Duke Arnold went on a pilgrimage to Rome and Palestine. During hi
2 CONC s absence, Catherine acted as regent.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She supported her son Adolf when he took over power from his father. Charles, Duke of Burgundy had Adolf taken capture in 1470, when he proved an unreliable ally to Burgundy.[3] Catherine spent her last years in Lobith, where she 
2 CONC died in 1476.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Issue=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mary (c. 1431-1463), who became Queen of Scotland by marriage to James II
2 CONT * William (born c. 1434), died young
2 CONT * Margaret (c. 1436-1486, Simmern), married on 16 August 1454 to Frederick I, Count of Palatine-Simmern.
2 CONT * Adolf (1438–1477)
2 CONT * Catherine (1439 – 1496), Regent of Guelders in 1477–1481.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Footnotes=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Marshall, Rosalind K. (2003). Scottish Queens, 1034-1714. Tuckwell Press. p. 57.
2 CONT * Kloek, Els (2013). 1001 Vrouwen uit de Nederlandse Geschiedenis. Vantilt. p. 71.
2 CONT * Kloek, Els (2013). 1001 Vrouwen uit de Nederlandse Geschiedenis. Vantilt. p. 71.
2 CONT
2 CONT *From: Wikipedia 
