2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Stamboom nr. :  209.252. Generatie :  18.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John I, Duke of Cleves''', Count of Mark (16 February 1419 – 5 September 1481). Jean de Belliqueux (warlike), was Duke of Cleves and Count of Mark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the son of Adolph I, Duke of Cleves and Mary of Burgundy.[1] He was raised in Brussels at the Burgundian court of his uncle Philip the Good. He ruled Cleves from 1448 from 1481, and Mark since 1461 after the death of his 
2 CONC uncle Gerhard who had waged war on his own brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT John fought 3 wars with the Electorate of Cologne and finally defeated Ruprecht of the Palatinate, conquering the cities of Xanten and Soest.[2] In these wars, he was supported by his uncle Philip the Good, bringing Cleves-Mark in
2 CONC to the Burgundian sphere of influence. His marriage with Elisabeth Countess of Nevers, from a sideline of the House of Burgundy, only strengthened this influence. John also took sides in the Münster Diocesan Feud supporting the as
2 CONC pirations of the House of Hoya to the episcopacy in Münster.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT John was also made a Knight in the Burgundian Order of the Golden Fleece in 1451,[4] with which he was depicted by Rogier van der Weyden. In 1473 he helped the Burgundian Duke Charles the Bold conquer the Duchy of Guelders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 April 1455, John married Elizabeth of Nevers, daughter of John II, Count of Nevers.[5] They had:
2 CONT
2 CONT John II, Duke of Cleves (13 April 1458 – 15 March 1521);[ married 3 November 1489 Matilda of Hesse Adolf (1461–1498); a canon of Liege
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbert, Count of Nevers (26 September 1462 – 21 November 1506); married 23 February 1489 Charlotte de Bourbon-Vendôme
2 CONT
2 CONT Dietrich (1464)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Cleves (1465–1513)
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Cleves[5] (1467–1505); Bishop of Nevers, Amiens and Autun
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikiwand.com/en/John_I,_Duke_of_Cleves
