2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismund I the Old
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismund I the Old (Polish: Zygmunt I Stary; Lithuanian: Žygimantas II Senasis; 1 January 1467 – 1 April 1548) of the Jagiellon dynasty reigned as King of Poland and also as the Grand Duke of Lithuania from 1506 until 1548. Earli
2 CONC er, Sigismund had been invested as Duke of Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of King Casimir IV Jagiellon and Elisabeth of Austria, Sigismund followed his brothers John I of Poland and Alexander I of Poland to the Polish throne. Their elder brother Ladislaus II of Hungary and Bohemia became king of
2 CONC  Hungary and Bohemia. Sigismund was christened as the namesake of his mother's maternal grandfather, Holy Roman Emperor Sigismund, who had died in 1437.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismund faced the challenge of consolidating internal power in order to face external threats to the country. During Alexander's reign, the law Nihil novi had been instituted, which forbade Kings of Poland from enacting laws wit
2 CONC hout the consent of the Sejm. This proved crippling to Sigismund's dealings with the szlachta and magnates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite this Achilles heel, he established (1527) a conscription army and the bureaucracy needed to finance it.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Janusz III of Masovia in 1526, he succeeded in annexing the Duchy of Masovia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Intermittently at war with Vasily III of Muscovy, starting in 1507 (before his army was fully under his command), 1514 marked the fall of Smolensk (under Polish domination) to the Muscovite forces (which lent force to his argument
2 CONC s for the necessity of a standing army). Those conflicts formed part of the Muscovite wars. 1515 he entered an alliance with the Holy Roman Emperor Maximilian I.
2 CONT
2 CONT In return for Maximilian lending weight to the provisions of the Second Peace of Thorn (1466), Sigismund consented to the marriage of the children of Vladislaus II of Bohemia and Hungary, his brother, to the grandchildren of Maxim
2 CONC ilian. Through this double marriage contract, Bohemia and Hungary passed to the House of Habsburg in 1526, on the death of Sigismund's nephew, Louis II.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Polish wars against the Teutonic Knights ended in 1525, when Albert, Duke of Prussia, their marshal (and Sigismund's nephew), converted to Lutheranism, secularized the order, and paid homage to Sigismund. In return, he was giv
2 CONC en the domains of the Order, as the First Duke of Prussia. This was called the Prussian Homage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismund's eldest daughter Hedwig (1513-1573) married Joachim II Hector, Elector of Brandenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT In other matters of policy, Sigismund sought peaceful coexistence with the Khanate of Crimea, but was unable to completely end border skirmishes. Sigismund was interested in Renaissance humanism and the revival of classical antiqu
2 CONC ity. He and his third consort, Bona Sforza, daughter of Gian Galeazzo Sforza of Milan, were both patrons of Renaissance culture, which under them began to flourish in Poland and in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Sigismund's death, his son Sigismund II August became the last Jagiellon king of Poland and Grand Duke of Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismund I was a member of the Order of the Golden Fleece.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigismund_I_the_Old#Marriages_and_issue
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriages and issue===
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1512, Sigismund married a Hungarian noblewoman named Barbara Zápolya, with whom he had two daughters:
2 CONT # Electress Hedwig of Brandenburg (1513–1573)
2 CONT # Anna Barbara died in 1515.In 1517, Sigismund married Bona Sforza, with whom he had:
2 CONT # Queen Isabella of Hungary
2 CONT # Sigismund II of Poland
2 CONT # Sophia, Duchess of Brunswick-Lüneburg
2 CONT # Anna I of Poland
2 CONT # Queen Catherine of Sweden
2 CONT # Wojciech Olbracht By his mistress, Katarzyna Telniczenka (d.1528), he also fathered three children out of wedlock:[3]
2 CONT # Jan (8 January 1499—18 February 1538), Bishop of Wilen (1519) and of Posen 1536
2 CONT # Regina (1500/01—20 May 1526), wed ca 20 October 1518 Hieronim von Szafraniec, Starost of Teschen (d.1556/59)
2 CONT # Katharina (Katarzyna) (1503—before 9 September 1548) wed after 1522 George II Count von Montfort in Pfannberg (d.1544)
