1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Princely count William IV of Henneberg-Schleusingen (c. 1475 – 24 January 1559), a member of the House of Henneberg, was a ruler of the Principality of Henneberg, within the Holy Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of William III of Henneberg, William inherited the Principality of Henneberg on 26 May 1480, when his father died, and reigned until his own death nearly eighty years later, on 24 January 1559.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Anastasia of Brandenburg, a daughter of Albert Achilles, Elector of Brandenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1543–1544 William embraced the Protestant Reformation.[2] In 1554 he signed a treaty of inheritance with John Frederick II, Duke of Saxony. William died in Salorno five years later. However, when his successor George Ernest, th
2 CONC e last Prince of Henneberg, died, both the Ernestine and the Albertine branches of the Wettin dynasty claimed his estates. In 1660 they were finally divided between the Ernestine duchies of Saxe-Weimar and Saxe-Gotha and the Alber
2 CONC tine Maurice of Saxe-Zeitz, while the lordship of Schmalkalden went to William IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel, honouring an inheritance treaty of 1360.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia Added by Janet Milburn 6/11/19
2 CONT
