2 CONT Joanna 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1455, Enrique married Princess Juana, sister of Afonso V of Portugal. After six years of marriage, in 1462, she gave birth to a daughter, Joanna, also Juana, nicknamed "La Beltraneja". Six years after the birth of the throne's 
2 CONC heir, part of the nobility of Castile revolted against the king. The rebels claimed that the princess was not the daughter of the king, but actually the daughter of Beltrán de La Cueva, 1st Duke of Alburquerque. This hypothesis wa
2 CONC s reinforced when the Queen had another child with the nephew of a Bishop. It is probable that the father of the first daughter of the Queen was the King, but is almost sure that the second one was not the King's. Townsend Miller 
2 CONC maintains that the question of her paternity cannot be answered. However, contemporary Historians assure Henry was impotent.
2 CONT
2 CONT The doubt of her legitimacy as an heir, the weakness of the king, the adultery of the queen, and the unruliness of the nobility all set the stage for a struggle for succession after Enrique's death. Enrique divorced his wife after
2 CONC  her scandalous behavior with Bishop Fonseca's nephew. After a long period of conflict between the rival factions, Enrique finally agreed to name Isabella his successor, in Guisando (Avila), provided she allow him to arrange her s
2 CONC trategic marriage. Isabella would go on to break this stipulation of the agreement.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1474 Henry IV of Castile was entombed at Santa María de Guadalupe, next to his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of the king war started in Castile. Juana was supported by Portugal, while the eventual winner, his half-sister Isabella of Castile had the support of Aragon via her husband Ferdinand and, later in the war, France.
2 CONC  Today, the succession of the Crown of Castile is still a polemic topic between historians.
2 CONT
