1 NOTE Ferdinand V (1452-1516), king of Spain, was the son of John II of Aragon. In 1469 he married Isabella of Castile and on the death of her brother Henry IV (1474) was proclaimed with her sovereign of Castile and Léon. On the death o
2 CONC f his father, he succeeded to Aragon (1479), and in 1512 he conquered the main part of Navarre, adding it to his realm. The conquest of the last Moorish kingdom in Spain (Granada) was undertaken (1482-92) with all the fervor of a 
2 CONC crusade, and the cruel persecution, spoilation, and expulsion of Jews (1490-92) was equally a matter of policy and a source of revenue. The establishment of the new form of Inquisition by the �Catholic kings� (1478), and the viola
2 CONC tion by them of their pledge of toleration to the conquered Moors, were popular in Spain, because they promoted the spiritual pride of orthodox Spaniards. When Columbus appealed to Ferdinand and Isabella for aid in his voyage (148
2 CONC 6-92) he found Ferdinand cool, but Isabella pleaded his cause, and Columbus obtained concessions and promises which, however, were never fulfilled. Ferdinand aimed at the formation of a great European coalition and in pursuance of
2 CONC  this idea he seized Naples, and outwitted or defrauded most contemporary potentates, including Henry VII of England. Consult Prescott�s History of the Reign of Ferdinand and Isabella. [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
1 NOTE Queen Isabel Ruled from 1474 to 1504. Daughter of Juan II de Castile and Leon. and second wife, Isabella of Portugal.
2 CONT In 1469 she married King Fernando de Aragon. She succeeded her brother, Enrique IV.
2 CONT There was trouble brewing though, because Alfonso of Portugal was supporting the claim of the throne of Spain of Juana la Beltraneja, the daughter of Enrique IV. As a result Alfonso of Portugal attacked Castile and Leon but was de
2 CONC feated by the Castillian army in 1476. Three years later her husband became King Ferdinando of Aragon. This matrimonial union between Ferdinando and Isabele was also the union of two main Spanish kingdoms and laid the foundation o
2 CONC f Spain's future.
2 CONT In 1478 Isabela I and her husband initiated the infamous Inquisition. They also finished what their ancestors had begun so many centuries before them, the reconquest of Spain from the Moors.
2 CONT They also were ruthless in their explusion of the Spanish Jews, both events happened in 1492.
2 CONT It was also in 1492 they sponsored Christopher Columbus' voyage to find a trade route to Asia. This later opened up a venue for setting up of an over seas colonial empire for Spain. It brought vast wealth and power to Spain at the
2 CONC  cost of many lives. Many of the old royal blood's descendants were called to serve. Many of them were sent to the New World.
2 CONT Ferdinand and Isabella were using the Feudal System to weaken the Feudal System to gain ultimate and unequivical power over their domain thus negating any possibility of revolt - coup de gras from their nobility.
