1 NOTE {geni:about_me} About Lucretia Oomkens van Ommeland and her ancestors / relatives "Oomkens van Ommeland and Oomkens von Esens".
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucretia Oomkens was the daughter of Jan Oomkens I (1699-1748), who was the "Ontvanger van het grote provinciaale kantoor te Groningen". Lucretia Oomkens' grandfather was Omius (Oomcke) Oomkens (1664-1753), who was Predikant of Wi
2 CONC ldervank for 63 years, where a handsome monument to his memory remains in the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT In earlier generations, this branch of the Oomkens family had moved from the Ommelanden to the city of Groningen, having converted to Protestantism and thus caused a family rift. Three generations owned a successful brewery in the
2 CONC  city, then the family took to Holy Orders in a big way. One author, writing in 1830 (M.J. Adriani) claims that Omius Jan Oomkens, recently deceased Predikant of Oudeschans, had been the 73rd Predikant in the family, a number whic
2 CONC h is gently deprecated, as being unfeasibly high, in the "Geslachttafels der Familie Oomkens". Another branch of the Oomkens family ran a well-known and successful publishing firm; books, maps and other publications published by t
2 CONC hem during the nineteenth century are still occasionally available. It is speculated that this "City" or "burgerlijke" branch of the family is what is called a "cadet" branch of the landed Oomkens family of the Ommeland-Oldambt an
2 CONC d East Frisia, on the basis that Willem Oomkens (c. 1535-1622), the descendant of Hero Oomkens von Esens ("the Younger") and Hero Oomkens von Stedesdorf ("the Elder"), Lords of Harlingerland in East Frisia, had two sons, Haye Will
2 CONC ems Oomkens and Oomke Willems Oomkens. They were known as Oomkens van Ommeland to differentiate them both from the "Groningen City" family and the earlier East Frisian ("von Harlingerland") generations. Haye Willem Oomkens' descen
2 CONC dants maintained their estates in the Ommeland-Oldambt (in Oostwold, Scheemda, Slochteren, Wagenborgen, Eexta and Beerta, among others) and we will mention them later. It has been speculated that Oomke Willems Oomkens' descendants
2 CONC  were the protestant "City" family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of Lucretia Oomkens' brothers at any rate went into the church as Predikanten, while a third, Jan Oomkens II (1746-1832) founded the family publishing firm. Jan's second wife (mother of his surviving children) was Harmanna Sth
2 CONC eemann (1751-1818). Harmanna was a cousin of the Oomkens van Ommeland (Oldambt) family via descendancy from the Siertsema family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucretia's husband died in 1791 in Essequebo. Lucretia lived until 1805 and died in Scheemda. Her family connections in the district, her sister-in-law's cousins, were either landed or in the Church. They included Oomke Oomkens (1
2 CONC 731-1814) of the Hoge Huis in Scheemda, Hillechien Oomkens (1740-1826) of the Pastorie te Eexta, and Jan Oomkens (1734-1817) of the Huningaborg Castle in Scheemda. These were the children of Willem Oomkens (1704-1781) of the Borg 
2 CONC (Castle) in Oostwold, out of his first wife Heike Jans Siertsema. It appears that Lucretia Oomkens, on becoming a widow, sought refuge and company with her landed Oomkens cousins in the Oldambt in the Groninger Ommelanden.
