1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moritz_von_Ortenburg 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://geneee.org/moritz/von+ortenburg?lang=de
2 CONT
2 CONT Moritz von Ortenburg († 6 July 1551 in Munich) was the third son of Imperial Count Ulrich II of Ortenburg and Veronika von Aichberg. He came from the powerful and respected Lower Bavarian noble family of Ortenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life and Career
2 CONT Moritz’s date and place of birth are unknown. Little is known about his youth, except that he was the third of five children. He had two older brothers and two younger sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moritz von Ortenburg made use of the improving relations between his house and the Wittelsbachs over the centuries, which enabled him to hold high offices in Bavaria. From 1529 to 1535, he was a member of the Great Committee for t
2 CONC he Rentamt (fiscal office) of Straubing, which surrounded the immediate imperial County of Ortenburg. In 1550, Moritz was appointed a privy councillor (Hofrat) to the Duke of Bavaria. Even before this, he had already been part of 
2 CONC the court entourage of Duke William IV. After William’s death, he joined the retinue of his son, Albert V.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wiguleus Hund wrote of Moritz von Ortenburg that he was held “in great favour” at the court of Duke William IV of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is assumed that these connections, along with his noble lineage, led to a proposed marriage arranged by Cardinal and Archbishop of Salzburg, Matthäus Lang von Wellenburg. The cardinal intended for Moritz to marry a relative of 
2 CONC his, the daughter of a knight from the von Trenbach family. However, Moritz showed no interest in entering into this marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6 July 1551, Moritz died unmarried and childless in Munich. His body was not transferred to Ortenburg or to the Ortenburg Chapel in Passau, but was instead buried in the Franciscan church in Munich.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first half of the 16th century, Moritz von Ortenburg played a part in the renewed rise of his noble house following its heyday in the High Middle Ages. His father Ulrich II and his uncle Christoph I had acquired great estat
2 CONC es and wealth in what is now Lower Bavaria and Upper Austria. Alongside them, Moritz gained political influence in the Duchy of Bavaria and helped to strengthen ties with the Bavarian ruling house. He thus contributed significantl
2 CONC y to the Ortenburgs once again becoming the second most powerful noble house in the duchy, and the wealthiest and most influential noble family in Lower Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, this renewed golden age was short-lived. Just twelve years after his death, the Imperial Counts of Ortenburg entered into renewed conflict with the Bavarian dukes. The trigger was Moritz’s cousin, Count Joachim I, who in 
2 CONC 1563 introduced the Protestant faith into the small county, sparking a decades-long conflict that led to the family’s renewed decline.
