1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Margaret of Lorraine, Duchess of Alençon (1463 at the castle of Vaudémont, Lorraine – 2 November 1521 in Argentan, Normandy) was a French noblewoman and a nun of the order of Poor Clares (Ordre des Clarisses). S
2 CONC he was beatified in 1921.'''[1]
2 CONT Life
2 CONT '''Margaret was born in 1463 in Vaudémont Castle, Lorraine as the youngest daughter''' of Frederick II, Count of Vaudémont and Yolande d'Anjou. '''She lost her father when she was the age of seven, and was brought up at Aix-en-Pro
2 CONC vence by her grandfather''' René of Anjou. '''When latter died in 1480, she was sent back to Lorraine to her brother''', René II. '''He arranged her marriage to René, Duke of Alençon, whom she wed in Toul on 14 May 1488.'''[2]
2 CONT '''Alençon and Margaret had three children:'''
2 CONT *Charles IV of Alençon (1489–1525), married Marguerite of Angoulême as her first husband.
2 CONT *Françoise of Alençon, Duchess of Beaumont (1490- 14 September 1550), married firstly in 1505, François, Duke of Longueville; married secondly in 1513, Charles de Bourbon, Duke of Vendôme, by whom she had thirteen children.
2 CONT *Anne, Lady of La Guerche (30 October 1492- 18 October 1562), married in 1508, William IX Palaeologos, Marquis of Montferrat by whom she had three children.
2 CONT '''Margaret died on November 2, 1521, in Argentan, Orne from natural causes.'''
2 CONT '''Almost 400 years after her death, she was beatified on March 10, 1921, by Pope Benedict XV.'''
2 CONT Widowhood
2 CONT '''Left a widow in 1492, she busied herself in the administration of her duchy and the education of her children. When she was relieved of the duties imposed upon her by her position, she decided to renounce the world and retired 
2 CONC to Mortagne, to a monastery of religious women who followed the rule of Saint Elizabeth. Later, having brought with her to Argentan some of these nuns, she founded there another monastery which she placed, with the authorization o
2 CONC f the pope, under the rule of Saint Clare, modified by the Minor Observants.'''[3]
2 CONT '''She herself took the religious habit in this house and made her vows on 11 October 1520. On 2 November 1521, after having lived an austere life for a year, she died in her modest cell, at the age of fifty-eight. Her body, prese
2 CONC rved in the monastery of the Poor Clares, and when that monastery was suppressed, was transferred to the church of Saint Germain d'Argentan. In 1793, during the French Revolution, it was profaned and thrown into the common burial 
2 CONC place.'''[3]
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT '''The memory of Margaret of Lorraine is preserved in the Martyrologium franciscanum and in the Martyrologium gallicanum. After an invitation made by the bishop of Séez, Jacques Camus de Pontcarré, Louis XIII asked Pope Urban VIII
2 CONC  to order a canonical inquiry into the virtues and the miracles of the Duchess.'''
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Lorraine
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''Margareta of Lorraine'''1,2
2 CONT Last Edited	29 Nov 2005
2 CONT F, #50087, '''b. 1463, d. 2 November 1521'''
2 CONT '''Father'''	Frederick VI, Count Vaudemont, Baron Joinville, Seigneur de Lambesc, Suse, Verbenne, Rumigny, & Boves1 b. 1417, d. 31 Aug 1470
2 CONT '''Mother'''	Jolanta de Anjou1,3 b. 2 Nov 1428, d. 23 Feb 1484
2 CONT '''     Margareta of Lorraine was born in 1463.1 She married Rene, 3rd Duke d'Alencon, Comte de Perche, Vicomte de Beaumont''', son of Jean II, Duke d'Alencon and Marie d' Armagnac, '''on 14 May 1488 at Toul, France.1,4 Margareta 
2 CONC of Lorraine died on 2 November 1521; Died a nun at Argentan.'''1,2
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT '''Rene, 3rd Duke d'Alencon, Comte de Perche, Vicomte de Beaumont''' b. c 1454, d. 1 Nov 1492
2 CONT '''Children	'''
