2 CONT Manuel I (Portuguese pronunciation: [mɐnuˈɛɫ]; Archaic Portuguese: Manoel I, English: Emmanuel I), the Fortunate (Port. o Venturoso), 14th king of Portugal and the Algarves (Alcochete, May 31, 1469 – December 13, 1521 in Lisbon) w
2 CONC as the son of Infante Fernando, Duke of Viseu, (1433 - 1470), by his wife, Infanta Beatriz of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was the granddaughter of King John I of Portugal; his father, Prince Fernando, was the second surviving son of King Edward of Portugal, being thus the cadet brother of King Afonso V of Portugal. Manuel succeeded his fir
2 CONC st cousin King John II of Portugal, who was also his brother-in-law, in 1495.
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel grew up among the conspiracies of the aristocratic high nobility against king John II. He watched many people being killed and exiled. His older brother Diogo, the duke of Viseu, had been murdered in 1484 by the king himsel
2 CONC f.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus, when receiving a royal order in 1493 to present himself to the king, Manuel had every reason to worry. Without reason: John II wanted to name him heir to the throne, after the death of his son, Prince Afonso, and the failed 
2 CONC attempts to legitimise Jorge, Duke of Coimbra, his illegitimate son. As a result of this stroke of luck he was nicknamed the Fortunate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel would prove a worthy successor to his cousin King John II, supporting the Portuguese exploration of the Atlantic Ocean and the development of Portuguese commerce. During his reign, the following was achieved:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1498 — Vasco da Gama discovers the maritime route to India
2 CONT
2 CONT 1500 — Pedro Álvares Cabral discovers Brazil
2 CONT
2 CONT 1505 — Francisco de Almeida becomes the first viceroy of India
2 CONT
2 CONT 1503-1515 — Afonso de Albuquerque, an admiral, secures the monopoly of the Indian ocean and Persian Gulf maritime routes for Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT All these events made Portugal rich on foreign trade whilst formally establishing its empire. Manuel used the wealth to build a number of royal buildings (in the Manueline style) and to attract scientists and artists to his court.
2 CONC  Commercial treaties and diplomatic alliances were forged with China and the Persian Empire. The Pope received a monumental embassy from Portugal during his reign, designed to be a show of the newly acquired riches to all Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Manuel's reign, royal absolutism was the method of government. The Cortes (assembly of the kingdom) only met three times during his reign, always in Lisbon, the king's seat. He reformed the courts of justice and the municipal c
2 CONC harters with the crown, modernizing taxes and the concepts of tributes and rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel was a very religious man and invested a large amount of Portuguese income to sponsor missionaries in their journeys to the new colonies, such as Francisco Álvares, and the construction of religious buildings, such as the Mo
2 CONC nastery of Jerónimos. Manuel also endeavoured to promote another crusade, against the Turks.
2 CONT
2 CONT His relationship with the Portuguese Jews started out well. At the outset of his reign, he released all the Jews who had been made captive during the reign of João II. Unfortunately for the Jews, he decided that he wanted to marry
2 CONC  Infanta Isabella of Aragon, then heiress of the future united crown of Spain (widow of his nephew Prince Afonso). Ferdinand and Isabella had expelled the Jews in 1492, and would never marry their daughter to the king of a country
2 CONC  that still tolerated their presence.
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1496, it was decreed that any Jew who did not convert to Christianity would be expelled from the country. However, those expelled could only leave the country in ships specified by the king. When those who chose expuls
2 CONC ion arrived at the port in Lisbon, they were met by clerics and soldiers who used force, coercion, and promises in order to baptize them and prevent them from leaving the country. This period of time technically ended the presence
2 CONC  of Jews in Portugal. Afterwards, all converted Jews and their descendants would be referred to as "New Christians", and they were given a grace period of thirty years in which no inquiries into their faith would be allowed; this 
2 CONC was later to extended to end in 1534.
2 CONT
2 CONT A popular riot in 1504 ended in the death of two thousand Jews; the leaders of this riot were executed by Manuel. In recent years, Portugal attempted to atone for the expulsion of the Jews. In a ceremony, Justice Minister José Edu
2 CONC ardo Vera Jardim called the expulsion of Portugal's Jews a black piece of our history. The state, he said, owes Jews moral reparation for centuries of brutal persecution.(New York Times, December 6, 1996).
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella died in childbirth in 1498, putting a damper on Portuguese ambitions to rule in Spain, which various rulers had had since the reign of Fernando I (1367-1383). Manuel and Isabella's young son Miguel was for a period the he
2 CONC ir apparent of Castile and Aragon, but his death in 1500 ended these ambitions. Manuel's next wife, Maria of Aragon, was also a Spanish princess, but not the oldest. This was Joanna of Castile, known as Joanna the Mad.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1506 the Pope Julius II gave Manuel I a Golden Rose.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jerónimos Monastery in Lisbon houses Manuel's tomb. His son João succeeded him as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Negotiations for a marriage between Manuel and Elizabeth of York in 1485 were halted by the death of Richard III of England. He went on to marry three times: first to Isabella of Aragon, princess of Spain and widow of the previous
2 CONC  Crown Prince of Portugal Afonso of Portugal; then he married another princess of Spain, Maria of Aragon (his first wife's sister); and then married Eleanor of Habsburg (niece of his first two wives) who after Manuel's death marri
2 CONC ed again to Francis I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. By Isabella of Asturias (October 2, 1470–August 28, 1498; married in 1497) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Miguel da Paz August 24, 1498 July 19, 1500 Prince of Asturias, Prince of Portugal and heir to both Portugal and Spain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. By Maria of Aragon (June 19, 1482–March 7, 1517; married in 1501) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince João (John) June 6, 1502 June 11, 1557 Who succeeded him as John III, 15th King of Portugal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Isabel (Elizabeth) October 24, 1503 May 1, 1539 Holy Roman Empress by marriage to Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Beatriz (Beatrice) December 31, 1504 January 8, 1538; Duchess of Savoy by marriage to Charles III, Duke of Savoy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Luís (Louis) March 3, 1506 November 27, 1555 Unmarried but had illegitimate descendants, one of them being António, Prior of Crato, a claimant of the throne of Portugal in 1580 (See: Struggle for the throne of Portugal). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Fernando (Ferdinand) June 5, 1507 November 7, 1534 Duke of Guarda and Trancoso. Married Guiomar (Guyomare) Coutinho, 5th Countess of Marialva and 3rd Countess of Loulé (?-1534). No surviving issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Afonso (Alphonse) April 23, 1509 April 21, 1540 Cardinal of the Kingdom. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Maria (Mary) 1513 1513   
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Henrique (Henry) January 31, 1512 January 31, 1580 Cardinal of the Kingdom who succeeded his grandnephew, King Sebastian (Manuel I's great-grandson), as Cardinal Henry, 17th King of Portugal. His death triggered the strugg
2 CONC le for the throne of Portugal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Duarte (Edward) October 7, 1515 September 20, 1540 Duke of Guimarães and great-grandfather of John IV of Portugal. Married Isabel of Braganza, daughter of Jaime, Duke of Braganza. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante António (Anthony) September 9, 1516 1516   
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. By Eleanor of Habsburg (November 15, 1498–February 25, 1558; married in 1518) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Carlos (Charles) February 18, 1520 April 14, 1521   
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Maria (Mary) June 18, 1521 October 10, 1577 Unmarried. 
2 CONT
