1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Margaret_Stewart_of_Scotland '''en.wikipedia.org....'''] - the free encyclopedia
2 CONT * Princess Margaret Stewart of Scotland
2 CONT ===Issue===
2 CONT # Margaret Crichton
2 CONT # Sir James Crichton
2 CONT * House  -	House of Stewart '''Margaret Stewart, Princess of Scotland (c. 1455 – ?)''' was a Scottish princess of the House of Stewart. She was the younger daughter of '''King James II''' and '''Queen Mary of Guelders''', a Dutch 
2 CONC princess by birth. Once engaged to the Lancastrian Prince of Wales, Margaret instead became the mistress of William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton (an enemy of her brother, James III), and the mother of his illegitimate daughter, Mar
2 CONC garet Crichton, later Countess of Rothes, and possibly his son, Sir James Crichton, progenitor of the Viscounts of Frendraught.[1] Princess Margaret and Lord Crichton may have been married later, after the death of Crichton's wife
2 CONC .
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT * Her mother, Mary of Guelders
2 CONT * Her father, James II
2 CONT * Princess Margaret was born between 1453 and 1460[2][3] in Scotland, the daughter of King James II of Scotland and Mary of Guelders. Her father's nickname was "Fiery Face" because of a conspicuous vermilion birthmark on his face 
2 CONC (possibly a "port-wine stain"). She had four brothers including James, who would succeed to the Scottish throne as James III in 1460 upon their father's accidental death by an exploding cannon. She had an elder sister, Princess Ma
2 CONC ry Stewart, who married twice; firstly to Thomas Boyd, 1st Earl of Arran; secondly to James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton. Margaret's mother died in 1463, leaving her an orphan at probably less than ten years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Her paternal grandparents were King James I of Scotland and Lady Joan Beaufort. Her maternal grandparents were Arnold, Duke of Guelders and Catherine of Cleves, daughter of Adolph IV, Duke of Cleves and Marie of Burgundy.
2 CONT ===Marriage proposals===
2 CONT * During the Wars of the Roses, Princess Margaret was briefly engaged to Edward of Westminster, Prince of Wales, the only son of Henry VI of England and Margaret of Anjou. However, the engagement was called off by her mother due t
2 CONC o political pressure from King Edward IV of England and Philip III, Duke of Burgundy. In 1476, she was proposed by James III to George Plantagenet, 1st Duke of Clarence, and she was afterward to have been married to Anthony Woodvi
2 CONC lle, 2nd Earl Rivers, brother-in-law of Edward IV; but neither of these alliances took place.[4]
2 CONT ===Later life===
2 CONT * William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton of Auchingoul (grandson of Lord Chancellor Crichton) is said to have "deliberately debauched Margaret" (James III's youngest and favourite sister), after discovering that his wife had been sed
2 CONC uced by the king.[5] Regardless of the truth of this story, Margaret did become Lord Crichton's mistress, which led to her disgrace and reputation for immorality and corruption. Their illegitimate daughter, also named Margaret, wa
2 CONC s born between 1478 and 1485 and raised in the royal court.[6] Princess Margaret may have had a son also, James Crichton, who married Catherine Borthwick, the eldest daughter of William, Lord Borthwick; however, James might have b
2 CONC een the son of Lord Crichton's wife.
2 CONT * Lord Crichton joined Margaret's brother Alexander Stewart, Duke of Albany in his rebellion against Margaret's eldest brother, the unpopular King James III. On behalf of the duke, Lord Crichton garrisoned his "very ancient and ma
2 CONC gnificent" castle of Crichton, for which his lands and titles were forfeited by the Parliament of Scotland in 1484, when Albany was sentenced for treason.[1] His castle was granted to a minion of James III, Sir John Ramsay of Balm
2 CONC ain.
2 CONT * According to historian George Buchanan (who was "always hostile"[7] to the Stewarts), Margaret had an incestuous relationship with her brother the king. Contemporary writers were unsympathetic in their descriptions of the prince
2 CONC ss. One writer calls her "a person, although young and beautiful, of depraved character, being even charged with too much familiarity with her own brother."[4] Another writer agrees, conceding that Margaret was "a Princess of grea
2 CONC t beauty, but of a reputation that was more than loose."
2 CONT ===Sir Walter Scott writes:===
2 CONT >"After garrisoning his castle and fleeing to England, William, the hereditary Lord of Crichton, pined in exile, from which he is said to have been recalled under the following circumstances: The Lady of Crichton, says Buchanan, d
2 CONC ied soon after her husband's flight to England; and the king, desirous to hide the disgrace of his favourite sister, who was almost frantic for the loss of her paramour, recalled Crichton from banishment, on condition of his marry
2 CONC ing the Princess Margaret. They were wedded accordingly, and Crichton seems to have obtained restitution of that part of his fortune which descended to him by his mother, the Barony of Frendraught, namely, in the North, which from
2 CONC  this time became his residence, and that of his successors. Crichton was reconciled to the king, and admitted to his presence at Inverness, during an expedition which James made towards the North near the conclusion of his reign.
2 CONC  He therefore entertained hopes of obtaining a full pardon, but neither of the brothers-in-law long survived the interview. Crichton died at Inverness, where, according to Buchanan, his monument was extant in the time of the histo
2 CONC rian. It seems uncertain whether the son who survived William Lord Crichton was the child of the Princess Margaret; but he left a daughter, their undoubted issue, who became Countess of Rothes."
2 CONT * If Scott's account is accurate, Princess Margaret, then Lady Crichton, may have spent the rest of her life at Crichton's residence "in the North", at the Barony of Frendraught.
2 CONT ===Descendants===
2 CONT * Her great-granddaughter, the beauty Lady Agnes Douglas, in 1599
2 CONT * Princess Margaret's daughter, Margaret Crichton, possibly had four daughters by her third husband, George Leslie, 4th Earl of Rothes, Ambassador to Denmark (whom she married twice), including Agnes Leslie, Countess of Morton, an
2 CONC d two sons, William Rothes and Norman Leslie, Master of Rothes whose rights of inheritance were forfeited as a result of both having been implicated in the murder of Cardinal Beaton in 1546. Their father, the 4th Earl of Rothes, w
2 CONC as tried and acquitted of the same crime. However, the mother of these children might have been a different wife of Rothes.
2 CONT * Princess Margaret's possible granddaughter Lady Margaret Leslie married Archibald Douglas, 8th Earl of Angus in 1575 and divorced him in 1587, probably because of her "infertility". (He remarried two weeks later.)[8] Another gra
2 CONC nddaughter, Lady Helen Leslie, had several children with Mark Kerr, the abbot of Newbattle, including Mark Kerr, 1st Earl of Lothian.[9]
2 CONT * Among her numerous possible descendants are included Diana, Princess of Wales and Camilla Parker-Bowles.
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________
2 CONT *'''Margaret Stewart1
2 CONT *'''F, #41405, d. after 1503
2 CONT *Father     James II Stewart, King of Scotland2,3 b. 16 Oct 1430, d. 3 Aug 1460
2 CONT *Mother     Marie of Guelders2,3 b. c 17 Jan 1433, d. 1 Dec 1463
2 CONT *'''     Margaret Stewart married William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton, son of James Crichton, 2nd Lord Crichton, Earl of Moray, Chamberlain of Scotland and Janet Dunbar, DID NOT MARRY; They had 1 illegitimate daughter (Margaret, w
2 CONC ife of William Todrig, of George Halkerston, & of George Leslie, 4th Earl of Rothes).1,4,5,6 Margaret Stewart and Sir George Plantagenet, Duke of Clarence, Great Chamberlain of England, Lord of Glamorgan, Morgannwg, & Richmond, Ea
2 CONC rl of Warwick & Salisbury were engaged in 1476; Marriage never took place.5,6 Margaret Stewart and Sir Anthony Wydevill, 2nd Earl Rivers, 8th Lord Scales, Lord of the Isle of Wight, Governor of Portsmouth, Sheriff of Caernarvonshi
2 CONC re were engaged circa August 1482; Marriage never took place.5,6 Margaret Stewart died after 1503.6
2 CONT *'''Family 1      Sir George Plantagenet, Duke of Clarence, Great Chamberlain of England, Lord of Glamorgan, Morgannwg, & Richmond, Earl of Warwick & Salisbury b. 21 Oct 1449, d. 18 Feb 1478
2 CONT *'''Family 2      Sir Anthony Wydevill, 2nd Earl Rivers, 8th Lord Scales, Lord of the Isle of Wight, Governor of Portsmouth, Sheriff of Caernarvonshire b. c 1442, d. 25 Jun 1483
2 CONT *'''Family 3      William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton d. b 23 Oct 1493
2 CONT *Child
