1 OCCU Reine de France (1515-1524), duchesse de Bretagne (9 January 1514), comtesse de Montfort-L'Amaury, de Blois, d'Étampes, d'Aast and de Coucy (9 January 1514), dame de Houdan and de Neaufles (9 January 1514)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_of_France
2 CONT
2 CONT and in French: http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_de_France_%281499-1524%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude of France (French: Claude de France, 14 October 1499 – 20 July 1524) was a princess and queen consort of France and ruling Duchess of Brittany, was the eldest daughter of Louis XII of France and Anne, Duchess of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the first spouse of Francis I of France, she was the mother of Henry II, and thus grandmother of the last three kings of the Valois line and also of Elisabeth, Queen consort of Spain; Claude, Duchess consort of Lorraine; and Ma
2 CONC rgaret, the Queen consort of Henry IV of France. She is also the maternal grandmother of Charles Emmanuel I, Duke of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Betrothals and marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Because her mother, Anne, Duchess of Brittany, had no surviving sons, Claude became heiress to the Duchy of Brittany. The crown of France, however, could pass only to and through male heirs, according to Salic Law. In 1504, Anne, 
2 CONC eager to keep Brittany separate from the French crown, effected the Treaty of Blois, which promised Claude's hand in marriage to the future Holy Roman Emperor Charles V with the promise of Brittany and the Duchy of Burgundy. The p
2 CONC rospect of a reduced France surrounded on several sides was unacceptable to the Valois, and so the betrothal was soon canceled.
2 CONT
2 CONT The French nobles argued against a betrothal to a foreigner, urging Louis XII to marry Claude to her cousin Francis, Duke of Angoulême, "who is at least all French", and was also the heir-presumptive to the French crown. In 1506, 
2 CONC the child was betrothed to Francis. In 1514, when her mother died, Claude became Duchess of Brittany; and on 18 May 1514, at Saint-Germain-en-Laye, she married Francis.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Court life
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude, the pawn of so much dynastic maneuvering, was short in stature and afflicted with scoliosis, which gave her a hunched back. She was eclipsed at court by her mother-in-law, Louise of Savoy, and her sister-in-law, the litera
2 CONC ry Marguerite, Queen consort of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Francis became King in 1515, two of Claude's ladies-in-waiting were the English sisters Mary and Anne Boleyn, and another was Diane de Poitiers. Mary became the king's mistress before returning home in about 1519. Anne served
2 CONC  as Claude's official translator whenever there were English visitors, such as in 1520. Anne was also a temporary companion to Claude's younger sister, Renée. Anne Boleyn returned to England in 1521, where she eventually became Qu
2 CONC een of England as the second wife of Henry VIII. Diane de Poitiers was a principal inspiration of the School of Fontainebleau of the French Renaissance, and became the lifelong mistress of Francis's son and successor, Henry II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude's life was spent in an endless round of annual pregnancies. Her husband had many mistresses, but was usually relatively discreet. Claude imposed a strict moral code on her own household, which only a few chose to flout.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] ChildrenClaude and Francis I had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louise, Princess of France (19 August 1515 – 21 September 1517) - died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charlotte, Princess of France (23 October 1516 – 8 September 1524) - died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Francis, Dauphin of France (28 February 1518 – 10 August 1536) - died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henry II, King of France (31 March 1519 – 10 July 1559) - married Catherine de' Medici. Had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Madeleine, Princess of France (10 August 1520 – 2 July 1537) - married James V of Scotland. No issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles of Valois, Duke of Orleans (22 January 1522 – 9 September 1545) - Died young. Had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margaret of France, Duchess of Berry (5 June 1523 – 14 September 1574) - married Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy in 1559. Had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death and later events
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude died in 1524, when she was only twenty-four. She was initially succeeded as ruler of Brittany by her eldest son, the Dauphin Francis, who became Duke Francis III, with Claude's widower King Francis I as guardian. After the 
2 CONC Dauphin's death in 1536, Claude's second son, Henry, Duke of Orleans, became Dauphin and Duke of Brittany. He later became King of France as Henry II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude's widowed husband himself remarried several years after Claude's death, to Eleanor of Habsburg, the sister of Emperor Charles V. The atmosphere at court became considerably more debauched, and there were rumours that King F
2 CONC rancis's death in 1547 was due to syphilis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Claude was named after St. Claude, a saint her mother had invoked during a pilgrimage so she could give birth to a living child.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] "Reine Claude" plum
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude is remembered in a classic small plum, the size of a walnut, pale green with a glaucous bloom. It is still called "Reine Claude" (literally, "Queen Claude") in France and is known in England as a "greengage".
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	9 January 1514 – 20 July 1524
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Anne
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Francis III
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen consort of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	1 January 1515 – 20 July 1524
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Francis I of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte of Valois
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis III, Duke of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Madeleine, Queen of Scots
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles, Duke of Orléans
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, Duchess of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	Valois-Orléans
2 CONT
