2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bâtard son of Johann II "the Babymaker", Duke of Cleves and Moeder van Judocus van der Beke
2 CONT * Besides his religious works, Joos van Cleve was an accomplished portrait painter.  He was so talented that the king of France at the time, Francis I, summoned him to Paris to work at the French court, during which time he painte
2 CONC d a number of portraits of the king, his wife, Eleanor of Austria and members of the court.  It is thought that Joos van Cleve also travelled to England around 1534.  This belief is based on a fact that he painted a portrait of th
2 CONC e forty-four year old monarch, Henry VIII, dated 1535, which is now part of the Royal collection.
2 CONT * [https://mydailyartdisplay.uk/2013/04/ '''He set up his own studio in Antwerp and over the next twenty years he took on a number of apprentices. His studio became the most famous in the city and he was inundated with painting co
2 CONC mmissions, many of which found their way to the leading European royal, ecclesiastic and merchant houses.''']  One of these lucrative commissions came in 1515, from the Nicasius and Georg Hackeney, wealthy merchants of Cologne, fo
2 CONC r a religious triptych which became known as the Death of the Virgin and which is currently housed in Cologne’s Wallraf-Richartz-Museum.  It was from this work that Joos also became known as Master of the Death of the Virgin. It w
2 CONC as works like this which brought the Flemish tradition to Cologne, and in so doing brought to bear an extensive influence on the Cologne school of art.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT *Married  (1) '''Anna Vijdt''' (married 1519; died 1528). She is believed to be the woman depicted in some of his works.
2 CONT * Married (2) '''Katlijne van Mispelteren''' (married 1531). She is known from a portrait attributed to Joos van Cleve, sometimes titled "Katlijne van Mispelteeren, The Artist's Wife". notes that his second wife, Katlijne, was lis
2 CONC ted as a widow in 1541, confirming his death around that time. 1541
2 CONT * Relationship:  Joos van Cleve also allegedly had an illegitimate daughter, Tanneken, from his relationship with '''Clara van Arp.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children with 1st wife Anna Vijdt'=
2 CONT
2 CONT He had two children from his first marriage, a daughter and a son. His son named Cornelis (1520) became a painter. Although the date of his death is unknown, Joos van Cleve drew up a will and testament on 10 November 1540, and his
2 CONC  second wife was listed as a widow in April. Little is known about his family life except that in 1519 Joos van Cleve met and married his first wife, Anna Vijts.  The couple had two children, both born in Antwerp.  The birth recor
2 CONC ds of the children show them listed as  “vander Beke, alias van Cleve” and that their father was registered as an Antwerp burgher which possibly indicates that Joos had registered as an Antwerp citizen in order to be able to work 
2 CONC in the city.  Joos and Anna had a son, Cornelis, who was born in 1520 and two years later in 1522, Anna gave birth to their daughter, Jozijne.  Their son,      In 1531, three years after the death of his first wife, Anna, Joos mar
2 CONC ried Katlijne van Mispelteren.  The couple had no children.   The exact date of Joos van Cleve’s death is uncertain but it is known that on November 10th 1540 he wrote his last will and testament and it is believed he died shortly
2 CONC  afterwards.  In April 1541 his wife, Katlijne, was listed as a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Cornelis vander Beke, alias van Cleve''' born in 1520 in Antwerp Flemish Region of Belgium . Cornelis became a talented portrait painter in his own right.  He helped his father in his workshop and would, after his father’s de
2 CONC ath, run the business   Cornelis was later known as Sotte Cleve (Mad Cleve) after becoming insane at the age of thirty-four. 
2 CONT * '''Jozijne vander Beke, alias van Cleve,''' born in 1522 in Antwerp Flemish Region of Belgium..
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Had a illegitimate daughter daughter relationship with Clara van Arp=
2 CONT
2 CONT Joos van Cleve also allegedly had an illegitimate daughter, Tanneken, from his relationship with Clara van Arp.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Tanneken vander Beke, alias van Cleve,''' born in Antwerp Flemish Region of Belgium.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joos_van_Cleve Joos van Cleve, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joos van Cleve (/ˈkleɪvə/;[1] also Joos van der Beke; c. 1485–1490 – 1540/1541) was a leading painter active in Antwerp from his arrival there around 1511 until his death in 1540 or 1541. Within Dutch and Flemish Renaissance paint
2 CONC ing, he combines the traditional techniques of Early Netherlandish painting with influences of more contemporary Renaissance painting styles.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT An active member and co-deacon of the Guild of Saint Luke of Antwerp, he is known mostly for his religious works and portraits, some of royalty. He ran a large workshop, with at least five pupils and other assistants, which produc
2 CONC ed paintings in a variety of styles over his career.[3] As a skilled technician, his art shows sensitivity to color and a unique solidarity of figures.[4] His style is highly eclectic: he was one of the first to introduce broad wo
2 CONC rld landscapes in the backgrounds of his paintings, sometimes collaborating with Joachim Patinir, which would become a popular technique of sixteenth century northern Renaissance paintings. Some works reflect the popular style of 
2 CONC Antwerp Mannerism, while others are variations on early Netherlandish masters of two or more generations before, or reflect recent Italian painting.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Four of his more important paintings have the monogram "JB", presumably for Joos van der Beke, rather inconspicuously placed. In three other works a self-portrait is placed among the minor figures.[6] From the seventeenth to the n
2 CONC ineteenth centuries, the name of Joos van Cleve as an artist was lost. Some of the paintings now attributed to Joos van Cleve were, at that time, known as the works of the "Master of the Death of the Virgin", after the triptych in
2 CONC  the Wallraf-Richartz-Museum in Cologne. In 1894 it was discovered that the monogram on the back of the triptych was that of Joos van der Beke, the real name of Joos van Cleve.[7] His oeuvre was reconstructed in the 1920s and 1930
2 CONC s by Ludwig von Baldass and Max Jakob Friedländer.[8] Now over 300 works are generally attributed to him or his workshop, which vary considerably in both quality and style.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the father of Cornelis van Cleve (1520–1567) who also became a painter, and inherited the workshop.[9] Cornelis became mentally ill during a residence in England and was therefore referred to as 'Sotte Cleef' (mad Cleef).[7
2 CONC ][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Altarpiece of the Lamentation, 1520–1525
2 CONT Joos van Cleve was born around 1485–90. The birthplace of Joos van Cleve is not precisely known. In various Antwerp legal documents he is referred to as 'Joos van der Beke alias van Cleve'. It is therefore likely that he came from
2 CONC  the Lower Rhenish region or city named Kleve (in traditional English "Cleves"), from which his name is derived. It is assumed that he began his artistic training around 1505 in the workshop of Jan Joest, whom he assisted in the p
2 CONC anel paintings of the wings for the high altar of the Nikolaikirche in Kalkar, Lower Rhine, Germany,[11] from 1506 to 1509. These include one of his self-portraits. From this a birth date of about 1485 to 1490 is inferred.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joos van Cleve is believed to have moved to Bruges between 1507 and 1511 since his painting style is similar to that of the painters of Bruges.[10] Later he moved to Antwerp, and in 1511 became a free master in the Antwerp Guild o
2 CONC f Saint Luke. He was co-deacon of the guild for several years around 1520, along with registering pupils there at various dates between 1516 and 1536.[7] In 1528 he bought a house from his wife's parents. As there are no records o
2 CONC f his being in Antwerp between 1529 and 1534, it is possible he spent some of this time in Italy or France at the court, or even London.[13][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT From surviving documents it is clear that he was alive in Antwerp on 10 November 1540 and dead by 4 February 1541.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Personal life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He had two children from his first marriage, a daughter and a son. His son named Cornelis (1520) became a painter. Although the date of his death is unknown, Joos van Cleve drew up a will and testament on 10 November 1540, and his
2 CONC  second wife was listed as a widow in April 1541.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT There are a number of other "van Cleef" Antwerp painters recorded from his time, some of whom may have been relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Work'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Adoration of the Magi triptych, apparently the prime version, Museo di Capodimonte, Naples, c. 1515
2 CONT Compositions were often copied, repeated or adapted; for example at least six versions of an Adoration of the Magi triptych composition by him and his workshop are known, though varying considerably in size, with the widths of the
2 CONC  centre panel ranging from 56 to 93 cm.[9] This probably reflected different intended sites for the paintings, from private house chapels to churches.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Numerous paintings contain heraldry, which often enables the customers to be identified, including eleven of the twenty-one altarpieces attributed to the workshop. In other works the identity of local saints gives clues. Antwerp w
2 CONC as the centre of European trade in the period, and the Antwerp merchant class was highly cosmopolitan. Five paintings can be linked with Italy, especially Genoa, and others to Cologne (three altarpieces) and Danzig in Germany, and
2 CONC  four to various Netherlandish cities. Others have the arms of his homeland Mark-Cleves, the territories of John III, Duke of Cleves, and the Holy Roman Empire, suggesting the duke or a close courtier. Three paintings delivered to
2 CONC  King Francois I of France are recorded.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT The great majority of his work is religious subjects or portraits, with the main exceptions being versions of the Suicide of Lucretia, and a Leonardo-esque half-length nude, the Mona Vanna in the National Gallery in Prague.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 2021 an episode the BBC Four series Britain's Lost Masterpieces, centred on the fine art collection of the Royal Pavilion in Brighton, uncovered a work of Joos van Cleve, a portrayal of Balthazar, previously attributed 
2 CONC to Bernard van Orley. The painting had once been the left-hand door of a folding altarpiece triptych.[17][18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Descent from the Cross, Rogier van der Weyden's figures (Prado), given a landscape background.[19]
2 CONT Descent from the Cross, Rogier van der Weyden's figures (Prado), given a landscape background.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Last Judgment, oil on panel, 123.8 cm (48.7 in) high
2 CONT The Last Judgment, oil on panel, 123.8 cm (48.7 in) high
2 CONT
2 CONT Salvator Mundi, c.1516-18, Louvre
2 CONT Salvator Mundi, c.1516-18, Louvre
2 CONT
2 CONT Annunciation; Italianate figure style, with the Early Netherlandish domestic setting of works such as the Mérode Altarpiece of Robert Campin, c. 1525
2 CONT Annunciation; Italianate figure style, with the Early Netherlandish domestic setting of works such as the Mérode Altarpiece of Robert Campin, c. 1525
2 CONT
2 CONT Rest on the Flight into Egypt, Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium
2 CONT Rest on the Flight into Egypt, Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrait of Eleonore of Austria, Queen of France, c. 1530
2 CONT Portrait of Eleonore of Austria, Queen of France, c. 1530
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Artistic influences'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The influence of Kalkar and Bruges are seen in many of Joos van Cleve's early works, such as Adam and Eve (1507). The Death of the Virgin (1520) shows the combined influence of several artists. It has the intense emotionality of H
2 CONC ugo van der Goes, and iconographic ideas of Jan van Eyck and Robert Campin. A strong influence of Italian art combined with Joos van Cleve's own color and light sensitivity make his works especially unique. The "Antwerp Mannerist"
2 CONC  style is identifiable in the Adoration of the Magi. It is thought that the "Antwerp Mannerists" were in turn influenced by Joos van Cleve.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like Quentin Matsys, a fellow artist active in Antwerp, Joos van Cleve appropriated themes and techniques of Leonardo da Vinci. This is apparent in the use of sfumato in the Virgin and Child. Multiple versions of a soft, sentiment
2 CONC al Madonna and Child and the Holy Family were discovered, produced in his workshop.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Royal portraits'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Joos van Cleve's skills as a portrait artist were highly regarded as demonstrated by a summons to the court of Francis I of France. There he painted the king (Philadelphia Museum of Art), the queen Eleanor of Austria (Kunsthistori
2 CONC sches Museum) and other members of the court.[7] His portrait of Henry VIII of England is of comparable size to that of Francis I (72.1 x 59.2 cm) and the compositions and costumes in both portraits are similar. Some historians ha
2 CONC ve interpreted this as evidence that the portraits were pendants painted to commemorate the meeting of the two kings in Calais and Boulogne on 21 and 29 October 1532, which Joos might possibly have witnessed. Other historians have
2 CONC  proposed the alternative view that van Cleve based the Henry VIII portrait on that of Francis I without meeting the English king. He may have hoped that this gesture might earn him English royal commissions in future.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Virgin and Child and Holy Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Holy Family, Metropolitan Museum of Art. Small devotional pictures of the Virgin Mary were the bread and butter work of early 15th century painting workshops, and Joos van Cleve produced many different types of the Virgin and 
2 CONC Child, the Holy Family and the Virgin and Child with St Anne. In some instances the prime version has been lost, but the type can be recovered through the numerous replicas produced by his workshop and copyists. Most of these were
2 CONC  no doubt produced with no specific commission, with many distributed by agents and dealers across Europe, for the houses of the wealthy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Holy Family was a newly popular subject in small devotional paintings, reflecting increased theological and devotional interest in the role of Saint Joseph. One of the earliest examples of the new type is the painting by Joos 
2 CONC dated to c. 1512 at the New York Metropolitan Museum of Art. This essentially reduced the figures from Jan van Eyck's Lucca Madonna (c. 1435, Städel, Frankfurt) to a close-up with domestic still life details, and added Saint Josep
2 CONC h over the Virgin's shoulder. The wine and fruits on the foreground are a reference to Christ's incarnation and future sacrifice. They also hint at the emerging genre of still-life painting in Flanders.[21][22]
2 CONT
2 CONT Another of the many compositional types exists in very similar versions, one in the Museum of Fine Arts, Budapest and another sold at Sotheby's on 30 January 2014. It is full of charm and tenderness and was popular in his own time
2 CONC  as well as with later collectors. The composition shows the Virgin with a brilliant red cloak, lined with fur and elaborately embroidered with pearls along the outside edge. The Virgin is seated in a loggia-like space with open w
2 CONC indows through which a distant mountainous landscape is visible. She has her lips parted in a slight smile while she helps the Christ Child drink from a glass with red wine, a symbol of Christ's future suffering and blood and the 
2 CONC Eucharist. Characteristic of Netherlandish painting of this period are the jewel-like colours and the details of the Virgin's costume and brocade pillow in the foreground.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT A new devotional type of the Virgin alone, her hands clasped in prayer, also appears in many versions. This may be called a Mater Dolorosa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Madonna and Sleeping Child in a Landscape, 1515–1520
2 CONT Madonna and Sleeping Child in a Landscape, 1515–1520
2 CONT
2 CONT Genoa, Palazzo Bianco
2 CONT Genoa, Palazzo Bianco
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonardo-esque Madonna of the Cherries, Berlin, c. 1525
2 CONT Leonardo-esque Madonna of the Cherries, Berlin, c. 1525
2 CONT
2 CONT Fitzwilliam Museum, c. 1525–30
2 CONT Fitzwilliam Museum, c. 1525–30
2 CONT
2 CONT With wine-drinking Child, sold Sotheby's, 2014
2 CONT With wine-drinking Child, sold Sotheby's, 2014
2 CONT
2 CONT The Virgin in Prayer, Minneapolis Institute of Arts
2 CONT The Virgin in Prayer, Minneapolis Institute of Arts
2 CONT
2 CONT The Virgin in Prayer, Yale
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Holy Family type found in many versions Saint Jerome
2 CONT
2 CONT Horizontally extended second type, by a follower of Joos Saint Jerome became increasingly of interest and popular after the publication of Erasmus's nine-volume set of his works, prefaced with a biography, in 1516, and the general
2 CONC  increased interest in the text of the Bible. Jerome had compiled the Latin Vulgate which remained the official version of the Western church until the Protestant Reformation was well under way.[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT Paintings of Saint Jerome were produced by Joos and his workshop, apparently beginning in 1521, in three basic types: penitent amid a desert landscape, with his attribute of a lion; at bust or half-length in a cluttered study, oft
2 CONC en with a skull on his desk; and lastly in his study, naked to the waist and holding a rock. The first two are not original, and borrow in particular from Albrecht Dürer; the last, from the 1520s onwards, is an unusual combination
2 CONC  of a figure type usually seen outdoors with the indoors study setting. In some versions of this type there are inscriptions referring to the Last Judgement. Various inscriptions in the second type, usually on the back wall, also 
2 CONC exhort thoughts of death and judgement. The last type is probably known only from numerous workshop or later versions.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Jerome in Penitence
2 CONT
2 CONT The second type described here
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Jerome in His Study, 1528, Princeton, with HOMO BULLA ("Man is a bubble") on the wall.
2 CONT
