0 @N6000000002188021118@ NOTE Barnim I, Duke of Pomerania
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Barnim I the Good (born c. 1217-1219, died December 13, 1278 in Altdamm) was a Pomeranian ruler from the Griffin dynasty. Son of Duke Bogislaw II, he succeeded to the Duchy of Pomerania-Stettin on his father's death in 1220; he sh
1 CONC ared the rule of Pomerania with his cousin Wartislaw III who resided in Demmin. In 1250 he became vassal of the margrave of Brandenburg. After the death of Wartislaw III in 1264, Barnim I united the whole of Pomerania under his ru
1 CONC le. He introduced German settlers and customs into the duchy, founded many towns, and was known for his generous ecclesiastical foundations.
1 CONT
1 CONT His eldest son Bogislaw IV was co-ruler from 1276 and sole ruler after Barnim's death, sharing power with Barnim's younger sons by his second marriage Barnim II and Otto I from the 1290s.
0 @N6000000002187820919@ NOTE Boleslaw V the Chaste
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaw WstydliwyBoleslaus the Chaste or the Shy (Polish: Boleslaw Wstydliwy) (21 June 1226 O.S. – 7 December 1279 O.S.) was the son of Leszek the White. Several years after the death of Henry the Pious at the battle of Legnica (
1 CONC 1241), he became king in Kraków, and thereby the predominant prince of the deeply partitioned Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married to Kinga or Cunegunda, daughter of the Hungarian king Béla IV. According to medieval chronicles the marriage was never consummated. Kinga, being extremely pious, was averse to fulfilling her marital duties. At first
1 CONC  Boleslaw tried to convince her to change her mind, but she did not accept. Then he reluctantly accepted the situation. His religious convictions forbade him to take a mistress. That is why he obtained the nick name "the Chaste" o
1 CONC r "the Shy".
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The Majestatic Seal of Boleslaw WstydliwyUnder his reign the town of Kraków, destroyed by the Tartars in 1241, was refounded, and mainly settled with German colonists. The new town was built according to a regular chess board patt
1 CONC ern (the "location" of 1257).
0 @N6000000002187828904@ NOTE Przemysl I of Greater Poland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Przemysl I (1220/21 – June 4, 1257), was a duke of Greater Poland (the provinces of Poznan, Kalisz, and Gniezno).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born to Ladislaus Odonic Plwacz, duke of Greater Poland, and Jadwiga of Pomerania, daughter of Msciwój I, duke of Eastern Pomerania.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Born: 1220 or 1221
1 CONT Realm of power:
1 CONT 1239-1247 Duke of Greater Poland, together with his brother Boleslaus the Pious
1 CONT 1247-1249 Duke of Poznan and Gniezno
1 CONT 1249-1250 Duke of Poznan and Kalisz
1 CONT 1250-1253 Duke of Greater Poland (Poznan, Gniezno and Kalisz)
1 CONT 1253-1257 Duke of Poznan
1 CONT Died 4 June 1257
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and childre
1 CONT He was maried 1244 to Elisabeth of Silesia, daughter of Henry II the Pious, duke of Silesia. They children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Przemysl II. 
1 CONT Konstancja (1245/6–1281), married 1260 to Conrad, Margrave of Brandenburg-Stendal. 
1 CONT Eufrozyna, Abbess of St.Clara in Trebnitz. 
1 CONT Anna (1253–after 26 June 1295), Abbess at Owinsk. 
1 CONT Eufemia (1253–1298), a nun at St.Clara, Breslau. 
0 @N6000000002188002792@ NOTE Margaret Sambiria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret SambiriaMargaret Sambiria (1230?-1282) was the Queen consort of Christopher I of Denmark, and acted as regent for Eric V of Denmark. In Danish she is also known as Margrethe Sprænghest.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was born daughter of duke Sambor II of the ruling family of Slavic (Vendic) Cassubians and his wife Mechtild of Mecklenburg. They held a lordship (then treated as duchy or principality) of so-called Pomerelia, in regions of Gd
1 CONC ansk in what later was known as west Prussia. Her birth is approximately in 1230.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was 1248 married to prince Christopher, the youngest son of Valdemar II of Denmark and Berengaria of Portugal. Her husband ascended the throne of Denmark in 1252 as Christopher I of Denmark by the then succession custom which 
1 CONC followed so called agnatic seniority and Margaret was crowned together.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her husband died 29 May 1259 (rumoredly poisoned). Their son and heir Eric V of Denmark who succeeded was a minor and Margaret was made regent, in which position she continued until 1264. Eric's succession overrode rights of sons 
1 CONC of earlier monarchs and was against the traditions of agnatic seniority. However, only such living were sons of king Abel, who had the fame of fratricide, murderer, and it was easier to dispossess them. King Christopher had all hi
1 CONC s reign made work to ensure his own line's succession. Although the accession of Eric led to serious rivalry for generations, Christopher's line was usually victorious and kept the kingship.
1 CONT
1 CONT She quarreled with lord Jakob Erlendsen and her husband's nephew Eric Abelson, as well as with counts of Holstein. After a loss in Lohede 1261 she together with her son the young king became imprisoned by Holsteins, but soon succe
1 CONC eded to liberty with help from Duke Albert of Brunswick. She took initiative. Margaret has a reputation of a very competent regent. Her nicknames "Spränghäst" and "Svarta Grete" are based on her strong will and energy. She died in
1 CONC  December 1282 andis buried in a Cistercian Church in Doberan in northern Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret and Christopher had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda (died 1311), married to Albert III, margrave of Brandenburg 
1 CONT Margaret (died 1306), married count John II of Holstein-Kiel 
1 CONT Eric, the future king of Denmark (1249-1286). 
1 CONT A never-ending rivalry between Eric and his supporters on one side, and the kin of former King Abel on the other, made Queen Margrethe write to the Pope in Rome around 1262 or 1263, asking the Pope to allow for women to inherit th
1 CONC e Danish throne, thus making it possible for one of Eric's sisters to become reigning Queen of Denmark in the event of the young King's death (he had no children as of yet). The Pope seemingly agreed.
0 @N6000000004533690409@ NOTE Margrethe Sambiria, -1282, Dronning, var en Datter afden østpommerskeFyrste Sambor, Broder til Svantopolk den store; hendes Moder hedMechtilde og stammede fra Meklenborg. 1248 ægtede hun Valdemar Sejersyngste Søn, Christoffer, og 
1 CONC 1252 blev hun kronet sammen med ham.Ægteskabet omtales som særdeles lykkeligt, og hun fødte sin Mand flereBørn, deriblandt 3 Sønner, af hvilke dog kun Erik (Glipping) naaedeManddomsalder. Saa
1 CONT længe Christoffer I levede, nævnes M. kun sjælden-, dog ses det, athun sammen med sin Fader har søgt at mægle mellem Kongen og ÆrkebispJacob Erlandsen. Efter sin Ægtefælles pludselige Død (29. Maj 1259)kom M. derimod til at spille
1 CONC  den største Rolle, i det hun somFormynderske styrede for den kun naarige Erik. Ethvert Haab om atmodstaa de talrige Fjender, Christoffer havde rejst imod sig, faldttil Jorden, da de sjællandske Bønder ved
1 CONT Næstved lede et knusende Nederlag over for Rygerfyrsten Jarimar, og M.maatte kjøbe Freden ved Indrømmelser til alle Sider; den fængsledeÆrkebisp frigaves, Erik Abelsøn blev forlenet med Sønderjylland. Snartslog M. atter ind paa de
1 CONC n Politik, hendes afdøde
1 CONT Mand havde fulgt; til Trods for Ærkebispens Indsigelse lod hun dejyske Bisper krone Erik i Viborg, og 1261 rykkede hun i Spidsen for enHær ind i Hertugdømmet. Hendes Fremgang var imidlertid kun kortvarig;Holstenerne kom til, og pa
1 CONC a Lohede (28. Juli) sejrede de fuldstændigover de danske; den unge Konge og hans Moder faldt selv i FjendernesHænder. Nu viste M. sin ubøjelige Energi; fra sit Fængsel i Hamborgkaldte hun Albert af
1 CONT Brunsvig (I, 167) til Hjælp; det lykkedes denne at faa Dronningenfrigivet, og i Forening vendte de tilbage til Danmark. Hertugen blevudnævnt til Rigets Forstander, og M. saa med Glæde, hvor voldsomt hanf6r frem mod Kongehusets Mod
1 CONC standere; hun skal endog selv, efter hvadder fortælles i den brunsvigske Rimkrønnike, have fattet Elskov tilden unge Hertug. 1263 forlod Albert igjen Danmark, men Aaret efterkunde M. i Steden hente sin egen Søn hjem, og 1266 blev 
1 CONC denne erklæretfor myndig.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ved denne Tid fik M. for Livstid overladt Estland som Enkeeje; endnuvedblev hun dog længe at være den virkelig styrende i hele Riget, oghendes Energi skyldtes det vel, at Ærkebispestriden førtes til en retlykkelig Udgang, ligesom 
1 CONC ogsaa, at Hertugdømmet
1 CONT efter Erik Abelsøns Død (1272) atter kom under Kronen. Senere træderhun mere tilbage, og endelig døde hun i Dec. 1282 under et Ophold iRostock; i det nærliggende Cistercienserabbedi i Doberan, hvortilallerede hendes Fader havde st
1 CONC aaet i venligt Forhold, fandt hun sitsidste Hvilested, og her vises endnu en mærkelig Træ- statue af hende,der i sin Tid har dannet Dækpladen over hendes Sarkofag.
1 CONT
1 CONT Til den Energi, M. udfoldede som Enke, svarer godt hendes TilnavnSprænghest; i Folkesagn kaldes hun ogsaa Sorte Grethe, men er herblandet sammen med sin store Navne, Margrethe Valdemarsdatter. Efternogles Mening skal det have være
1 CONC t M., der har bygget den Del afDanevirke, som kaldes Margrethevolden, hvad der dog er lidet rimeligt.Til det hellige Korses Kloster i Rostock skjænkede M. noget Gods;snart efter hendes Død vidste man at fortælle, at det var hende,
1 CONC  derhavde stiftet Klosteret og givet det en Splint af det hellige Kors,som hun personlig havde modtaget som Gave af Pavens Haand, og somunder Hjemrejsen havde reddet hende og hendes Følge i stor Havsnød;alt dette fremstilledes i e
1 CONC t Dokument, der foregaves udstedt afDronningen selv, men hvis Uægthed maa anses for sikker.
1 CONT
1 CONT Script, rer. Prussicarum I, 690 f. Videnskab. Selsk. Skr., Hist. phil.Afd.,
1 CONT 5. R. IV, 377 ff. Aarh. f. nord. Oldkynd. 1877, S. 55 ff.; 1881, S. 50ff.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kr. Erslev.
0 @N6000000007329033176@ NOTE Kung av Danmark.
0 @N6000000002188021150@ NOTE Barnim II, Duke of Pomerania
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Barnim II (circa 1277–28 May 1295) was Duke of Pomerania.
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Duke Barnim I and his third wife, Mechtild of Brandenburg, Barnim's father died in 1278 when he was still a child. He was titular co-ruler with younger brother Otto I and his much older half-brother Bogislaw IV, in whose ha
1 CONC nds effective power lay. Protected by his mother, Barnim had no real power until 1294 when he and Otto gained a shared in ruling Pomerania.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was killed the following year, in the Ueckermünder Heide, by an irate husband whose wife he had seduced. He left no children and Pomerania was ruled by Bogislaw and Otto after his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT   This biography of a European noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reference
1 CONT (German) Werner Buchholz (ed.), Deutsche Geschichte im Osten Europas. Pommern. Siedler Verlag, Berlin 1999, ISBN 3-88680-272-8 
0 @N6000000002188021160@ NOTE Otto I, Duke of Pomerania
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto I (1279 – 31 December 1344) was Duke of Stettin and Duke of Pomerania.
1 CONT
1 CONT Youngest, and probably posthumous, son of Duke Barnim I and his third wife, Mechtild of Brandenburg, Otto became titular co-ruler at his birth, along with his elder half-brother Barnim II and his much older half-brother Bogislaw I
1 CONC V.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bogislaw was effectively sole ruler while Barnim and Otto were children, and it was not until 1294 that he shared power with his younger half-brothers. In 1295, with Barnim dead, the brothers divided Pomerania with Otto as ruler o
1 CONC f Stettin while Bogislaw received Wolgast.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1296 Otto married Elizabeth of Holstein. Their children included Barnim III, future Duke of Pomerania, and Mechtild, who married Johann III, Lord of Werle.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT From 1320 his son Barnim (III) was co-ruler with Otto.
0 @N6000000002188305112@ NOTE Bogislaw IV, Duke of Pomerania
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Bogislaw IV (died 19 February 1309 or 24 February 1309) was Duke of Pomerania for thirty years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bogislaw was the eldest son of Duke Barnim I by his first wife, sometimes said to be a daughter of King Eric X of Sweden. He was an adult at his father's death in 1278, having been co-ruler since 1276. His half-brothers Barnim II 
1 CONC and Otto I were still very young and was sole ruler in Pomerania during their minority.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the early 1280s Bogislaw was involved in the war in the neighbouring margraviate of Brandenburg. At this time he married Mechtild, daughter of Johann I and a sister of reigning Margrave Otto IV, but all of his known children we
1 CONC re by a second marriage to a daughter of the Duke of Rügen. Their son Wartislaw IV would later rule in West Pomerania and inherit Rügen.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 1290s with his brothers now adults, Pomerania was divided between them with Bogislaw receiving Wolgast as his share. Barnim II died soon afterwards and Otto ruled Stettin.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 1300s he was allied with would-be King of Poland Wladyslaw the Short. He was unsuccessfully involved in the opening stages of the decade-long Brandenburg War. He died in 1309 leaving his share of Pomerania to his son Warcis
1 CONC law.
0 @N6000000002322315380@ NOTE She was the daughter of Boleslaw the Pious of Great Poland.
