0 @N6000000000945043972@ NOTE Dronning av Norge 1299 - 1312. 
1 CONT Hun var grevedatter fra Nord-Tyskland, og Håkons firmenning. Hennes far var fyrst
1 CONT Wizlaw II av Rügen.
1 CONT Under parets kroning i Oslo tok overdådigheten henimot orientalske former. Man
1 CONT kunne, etter de islandske annalene, gå de omkring 700 metrene ute fra Ørene til
1 CONT Hallvardskirken på ?dunklær, kostbar silke og baldakin?.
1 CONT Fyrst Wizlaw, kong Håkons svigerfar, var i Norge i anledning festemålet mellom kong
1 CONT Håkons datter Ingebjørg og hertug Erik. Hans testamente ble da oppsatt og datert
1 CONT 27.12.1302, et sammendrag er trykt i ?Regesta Norvegica?, Bind III 1301-1319,  nr. 66:
1 CONT ?Testament for fyrst Wizlaw II av Rügen. Følgende punkter angår Norge:
1 CONT Fyrsten ønsker å bli gravlagt i Mariakirken, som får 100 mark rent sølv norsk vekt til
1 CONT byggearbeider. Kollegiet ved kirken får 100 norske mark for å lese messe og gi mat til 50 fattige
1 CONT på årsdagen for hans død. Hallvardskirken får 100 norske mark for at det skal leses messe på
1 CONT årsdagen hans både der og i alle sognekirkene i Oslo. Han gir mindre pengesummer til ulike
1 CONT geistlige institusjoner i Oslo: dominikanerne, fransiskanerne, Hovedøy kloster, Nonneseter
1 CONT kloster, Nikolaskirken, Klemenskirken, Korskirken og St Laurentiushospitalet. Ellers får
1 CONT dronning Eufemia 2 store sølvboller, en gave fra kongen av Sverige, en annen datter 6 små
1 CONT sølvboller som var en gave fra dronning Eufemia, og en tredje datter som oppholder seg hos
1 CONT den norske dronningen får 200 slaviske mark. Hertug Henrik av Mecklenburg får et forgylt
1 CONT sølvbelte som er en gave fra den norske kongen. Testamenteksekutorer er kong Håkon,
1 CONT hertug Erik av Sverige, hertug Valdemar av Sverige og herr Erik av Langeland. Vitner: kong
1 CONT Håkon, dronning Eufemia, hertug Erik av Sverige, grev Jacob av Halland, hertug Eriks
1 CONT drottsete Arnbjørn Sigstensson, kong Håkons kansler Åke, fyrst Wizlaws kapellan og sekretær
1 CONT Lambert Pralenberg og andre geistlige og lekmenn.
1 CONT Beseglet av fyrst Wizlaw og hans kapellan Lambert Pralenberg.?
1 CONT Original (latin pergament): Staatsarchiv Stettin (Mser I 33) nr 125 fol 41 v (Codex
1 CONT Rugianus).
1 CONT 10.12.1305 ble følgende vitnebrev utferdet i Bergen (DN III 61):
1 CONT
1 CONT Fra ?Regesta Norvegica?, Bind III 1301-1319,  nr. 307:
1 CONT ?Vitnebrev fra erkebiskop Jørund av Nidaros, biskopene Erlend av Færøyene,
1 CONT Kjetil av Stavanger, Arne av Bergen og Ingjald av Hamar, Bjarne Lodinsson, Weyland av
1 CONT Sticklaw, Åke kansler, Snare Aslaksson, Erling Åmundesson, Salomon, kannik i Nidaros,
1 CONT Gibbun marskalk og Bjarne Audunsson: Var tilkalt av kongen i Breidastova i Bergen da kong
1 CONT Håkon [Magnusson] kunngjorde at han hadde mottatt hele dronning Eufemias medgift, 3000
1 CONT mark kølnsk vekt, av fyrst Wizlaw, hvorav 2000 mark var utbetalt som lån til hertug Valdemar
1 CONT av Sønderjylland mot den sikkerhet som tidligere er stilt (Jfr nr 228). Kongen fornyet også med
1 CONT utstedernes samtykke dronningens tilgave, nemlig Bygdøy ved Oslo med alt som tilhører den,
1 CONT og som fast inntekt 1200 (tiu hundrad) mark verd 8 ørtoger smør og andre gamle
1 CONT betalingsmidler av vissøren på de steder han bestemmer i egne brev og det er best for henne,
1 CONT på de betingelser som hans tidligere brev om dette fastsetter (nr 306). Utstederne lover å
1 CONT overholde disse bestemmelsene.
1 CONT Beseglet av utstederne.?
1 CONT Avskrift (papir): Det kongelige Bibliotek, København.
0 @N6000000006127699281@ NOTE Eufemia, norsk drottning. Grevinna av Ruppin. Gift med Håkan V av Norge. Var dotterdotter till furst Wizlaw II av Rügen.Barn:1. Ingeborg Håkansdotter av Norge 
0 @N6000000004533690102@ NOTE Til Aagaard og Restrup
0 @N6000000004533757073@ NOTE <b>Erik</b> (Langben), -1310, Herre til Langeland, yngre Søn af HertugErik (I) af Sønderjylland. Han nævnes første Gang som Drosten SkjalmStigsens Drabsmand (23. Avg. 1292). Drabet fandt Sted om Natten iKongens Gaard og var uden a
1 CONC l Tvivl fremkaldt ved
1 CONT det Fjendskab, som opstod, da Erik Menvæt (Menved) efter at værebleven myndig inddrog Langeland og Hertugslægtens Ejendomme paa Fyn(Svendborg o. a.), som vare tilfaldne E. i Arv ligesom fordum hansFarbroder Abel (I, 34). Hertug Va
1 CONC ldemar og E. Langben sloge sig fra nuaf til Kongens Fjender, og Aaret efter kom det til et Sammenstød paaSommersted Hede
1 CONT (ved Haderslev), der dog endtes med et Forlig, ved hvilket Langelandblev givet i Forlening til Junker E. Han ægtede nu Sophie afRosenburg, en Datter af Borggrev Burchard og Jutta, Erik PlovpenningsEnke. Hun var saaledes Halvsøster
1 CONC  til den afdøde Dronning Ingeborg afNorge, Moder til Kong Erik (Præstehader), saa vel som til Agnes ogJutta, der vare gaaede i St. Agnetes Kloster i Roskilde, men senereforlode det og døde i Sverige. Dette Giftermaal
1 CONT bragte E. Langben i Forbindelse med Kongen af Norge, som slog ham tilRidder, og i mange Stridigheder med Erik Menvæt, som bl. a.tilbageholdt hans Hustrus Arv efter Søstrene. Det kom til et Forlig iVordingborg 3. Febr. 1296, ved hv
1 CONC ilket Kongen forpligtede sig til atudlevere denne Arv, medens Junker Abels efterladte Gods paa Fyntildømtes Kongen som lovlig erhvervet fra Datteren. Kun Rudkjøbingblev ved denne Lejlighed lagt til Tranekjær Len;
1 CONT E. stadfæstede samme Dag Stadens Friheder.
1 CONT
1 CONT I de følgende Aars Stridigheder mellem Erik Menvæt og Nordmændene iForening med de fredløse træffes Junker E. snart paa den ene, snartpaa den anden Side. Han døde 1310. Hans Hustru synes at have beholdtTranekjær som Enkesæde; her 
1 CONC bortskjødede hun i Kongens og mangeStormænds Nærværelse Arven efter sine Søstre til St. Agnetes Kloster;kun Skjelskør forbeholdt hun sig selv (1315). Der fortælles endvidere, at hun kort efter Mandens Død overleverede Kongen et Br
1 CONC ev medmange Stormænds Segl, som hun havde fundet i hans Brevskrin; detindeholdt en Sammensværgelse imod Kongen. Denne lod Hovedmændene kaldetil et Møde i Vordingborg, dømme til Døde og henrette. E. og Sophieefterlode ingen Børn.
1 CONT
1 CONT <i>A. D. Jørgensen.</i>
0 @N6000000002322315402@ NOTE Brother-in-law to Charles I, aka Charles Robert, King of Hungary. Casimir III
1 CONT was called "the Great" because he was deemed a peaceful ruler, a "peasant
1 CONT king," and a skillful diplomat. Through astute diplomacy he annexed lands
1 CONT from western Russia and eastern Germany. Domestically he unified the
1 CONT government, codified its written law, endowed new towns with self-government
1 CONT of the Magdeburg Law, and founded Poland's first university. See message
1 CONT under wife number two.
0 @N6000000002188002541@ NOTE Casimir III of Poland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Parents Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high, 
1 CONT Jadwiga Kaliszka, of Gniezno and Greater Poland
1 CONT
1 CONT Consorts Aldona Ona, Adelheid of Hessen, Christina, Jadwiga of Glogow and Sagan 
1 CONT Children 5 daughters 
1 CONT Date of Birth 1310 
1 CONT Place of Birth Cracow 
1 CONT Date of Death 1370 
1 CONT Place of Death Cracow 
1 CONT Ruled 1333 – 1370 
1 CONT
1 CONT Casimir III, called the Great (Polish: Kazimierz Wielki; 1310 – 1370), King of Poland (1333-70), was the son of King Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high and Jadwiga of Gniezno and Greater Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Biography 
1 CONT 1.1 The Great King 
1 CONT 1.2 Concession to szlachta 
1 CONT 1.3 Relationship with Polish Jews 
1 CONT 2 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT Born in Krakow, Kazimierz the Great married first Anna, or Aldona Ona, the daughter of the prince of Lithuania, Gediminas. Their daughters were Cunigunda (d 1357), who was married to Louis VI the Roman, the son of Louis IV, Holy R
1 CONC oman Emperor, and Elisabeth, who was married to Duke Bogislaus V of Pomerania. Kazimierz then married Adelheid of Hessen, and this was the start of his bigamous marriage career. He divorced Adelheid in 1356, married a lady named C
1 CONC hristina, divorced her, and fourth (when at least Adelheid and possibly also Christina were alive) c. 1365 married Jadwiga of Glogow and Sagan.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Polish 50-zloty banknote with image of Kazimierz the Great.His three daughters by his fourth wife were very young and regarded as of dubious legitimacy because of their father's bigamy. By an unfortunate statistical fluke, all of 
1 CONC the 5 children he fathered with his first and fourth wife were daughters, and so he was unable to produce a lawful male heir to his throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Kazimierz, the last Piast king of Poland, died in 1370, his nephew King Louis I of Hungary succeeded him to become king of Poland in personal union with Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT The Great King
1 CONT
1 CONT Sarcophagus of Kazimierz the Great at Wawel Cathedral.Kazimierz is the only Polish king who both received and has kept the title of the Great in Polish history (Boleslaw I Chrobry was once also called the Great, but not today), an
1 CONC d the title is well deserved. When he received the crown, his hold on it was in danger, as even his neighbours did not recognise his title and instead called him "king of Kraków". The economy was ruined, and the country was depopu
1 CONC lated and exhausted by war. Upon his death, he left a country doubled in size (mostly through the addition of land in today's Ukraine, then the Duchy of Halicz), grew prosperous, wealthy and had great prospects for the future. Alt
1 CONC hough he is depicted as a peaceful king in children's books, he in fact waged many victorious wars and was readying for others just before he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Royal seal of Kazimierz the Great.Kazimierz the Great built many new castles, reformed the Polish army and Polish civil and criminal law. At the Sejm in Wislica, March 11, 1347, he introduced salutary legal reforms in the jurispru
1 CONC dence of his country. He sanctioned a code of laws for Great and Little Poland, which gained for him the title of "the Polish Justinian"; and he also limited the interest rate charged by Jewish money-lenders to Christians to 8 % p
1 CONC er annum, a rate of 108-180% being previously common. (Owing to extremely high and unstable inflation rates, significantly lower interest rates would result in net losses for the lender. For example, in 1264 the King of Austria ha
1 CONC d capped Jewish money-lenders' interest rates at 8 dinars on the talent, approximately 170% at the time). This measure was passed after consistent pressure by the szlachta of the Sejm (who were primary clients of Jewish money-lend
1 CONC ers). This measure was to the detriment of the King, who later affirmed that Jews be allowed to loan on interest as "property" of the King, in order to supplement the King's income when needed. He founded the University of Kraków,
1 CONC  although his death stalled the university's development (which is why it is today called the "Jagiellonian" rather than "Casimirian" University).
1 CONT
1 CONT He organized a meeting of kings at Kraków (1364) in which he exhibited the wealth of the Polish kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Concession to szlachta
1 CONT In order to enlist the support of noblemen (szlachta), especially the military help of pospolite ruszenie, Kazimierz was forced to give up important privileges to their caste, which made them finally clearly dominant over townsfol
1 CONC k (burghers or mieszczanstwo).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT 14th-century wiec, in the reign of Kazimierz the Great.
1 CONT In 1335, in the "treaty of Trencín", Kazimierz relinquished "in perpetuity" his claims to Silesia. In 1355 in Buda Kazimierz designated Louis of Anjou (Louis I of Hungary) as his successor. In exchange, the szlachta's tax burden w
1 CONC as reduced and they would no longer be required to pay for military expeditions expenses outside Poland. Those important concessions would eventually lead to the ultimately crippling rise of the unique nobles' democracy in the Pol
1 CONC ish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second daughter, Elisabeth, Duchess of Pomerania, bore a son in 1351, named Kazimierz of Pomerania after his maternal grandfather. He was slated to become the heir, but did not succeed to the throne, dying childless in 1377, 7
1 CONC  years after King Kazimierz. He was the only male descendant of King Kazimierz who lived during his lifetime.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also, his son-in-law Louis of Bavaria-Brandenburg was thought as a possible successor. However, he was not deemed very capable, and his wife had died already in 1357, without children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kazimierz had no sons. Apparently he deemed his own descendants either unsuitable or too young to inherit. Thus, and in order to provide a clear line of succession and avoid dynastic uncertainty, he arranged for his sister Elisabe
1 CONC th, Dowager Queen of Hungary, and her son Louis king of Hungary to be his successors in Poland. Louis was proclaimed king on Kazimierz's death in 1370, and Elisabeth held much of the real power until her death in 1380.
1 CONT
1 CONT Many of the influential lords of Poland were unsatisfied with the idea of any personal union with Hungary, and 12 years after Kazimierz's death, (and only a couple of years after Elisabeth's), they refused in 1382 to accept the su
1 CONC ccession of Louis's eldest surviving daughter Mary (Queen of Hungary) in Poland too. They therefore chose Mary's younger sister, Hedvig, as their new monarch, and she became "King" (=Queen Regnant) Jadwiga of Poland, thus restorin
1 CONC g the independence enjoyed until the death of Kazimierz, twelve years earlier.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Relationship with Polish Jews
1 CONT King Kazimierz was favorably disposed toward Jews. On October 9, 1334, he confirmed the privileges granted to Jewish Poles in 1264 by Boleslaus V. Under penalty of death, he prohibited the kidnapping of Jewish children for the pur
1 CONC pose of enforced Christian baptism. He inflicted heavy punishment for the desecration of Jewish cemeteries.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Jews had lived in Poland since before the reign of King Kazimierz, he allowed them to settle in Poland in great numbers and protected them as people of the king.
