0 @N6000000002188017857@ NOTE Henry the Mild, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry (Latin Henricus, died 3 December 1416), Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, called Henry the Mild, was prince of Lüneburg from 1388 to 1409 jointly with his brother Bernard I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, from 1400 to 1409 also of Wo
1 CONC lfenbüttel, and from 1409 until his death sole prince of Lüneburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was the fourth son of Magnus with the Necklace, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. He participated in the prosecution of the murderers of his brother Frederick, elected King of the Romans, after 1400. In 1404, Henry was kidnapped b
1 CONC y Bernard, Count of Lippe; when he paid a ransom, he was released, and later, with the support of Emperor Rupert, took revenge on Bernard.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry married Sophie, daughter of Wratislaus VI, Duke of Pomerania, in 1388. Children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * William (c. 1392 – 1482)
1 CONT     * Catherine (died 1442), married Frederick I, Elector of Saxony
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry married Margaret (c. 1389 – c. 1446), daughter of Herman, Landgrave of Hesse, in 1409. They had one known child:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Henry (c. 1411 – 1473)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * (German) Zedlers Universal-Lexicon, vol. 12, p. 775-776
1 CONT     * (German) Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie, vol. 11, p. 486-488
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Wenceslaus Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Prince of Lüneburg
1 CONT 1388–1416 Succeeded by
1 CONT William the Victorious
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Frederick I Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Prince of Wolfenbüttel
1 CONT 1400–1409 Succeeded by
1 CONT Bernard I
0 @N6000000002188017839@ NOTE Frederick, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick (circa 1357 – 5 June 1400, Kleinenglis), Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, was ruler of the Principality of Brunswick from 1373, and, according to some sources, briefly German king-elect in opposition to Wenceslaus in 1400.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick was the eldest son of Magnus II, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg and Catherine of Anhalt-Bernburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick was underage until 1381, until which time Otto the Evil of Brunswick-Göttingen was his guardian. Like his father, he was thrust into the Lüneburg Succession War, which he and his brothers attempted to end in 1373 by a tr
1 CONC eaty with the Ascanian dukes of Saxony-Wittenberg. According to this treaty, the rule over the Principality of Lüneburg would alternate between the two families. But the dispute continued; together with his brothers, Frederick eve
1 CONC ntually won the war by conquering Lüneburg itself in 1388.
1 CONT
1 CONT In May of 1400, Frederick took part in an assembly of the princes of the Holy Roman Empire in Frankfurt; the purpose of the meeting was to discuss the deposition of Wenceslaus, King of the Romans. According to legend, Frederick wa
1 CONC s elected as an anti-king by a subset of the princes; because no agreement over his election could be reached, he left the assembly. Modern historians are at odds with each other whether Frederick was ever considered a candidate o
1 CONC r even elected, since there is no documentary evidence for this. It is undisputed that he was murdered during his journey home by Henry VII, Count of Waldeck. The murder may have served to get rid of a royal candidate; or the stor
1 CONC y of the royal election may have sprung up to find a motive for the murder.
1 CONT
1 CONT The murder caused much concern at the time; a stone cross was erected at its location in Kleinenglis near Fritzlar. Frederick was entombed in Brunswick Cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick married Anna (died 1388), a daughter of Wenceslaus I, Duke of Saxony-Wittenberg, in 1386. With her he had two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Catherine (died circa 1439), married Henry XXIV, Count of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen
1 CONT     * Anna (died 1432), married Frederick IV, Duke of Austria
0 @N6000000006127688667@ NOTE Sophia av Braunschweig-Lüneburg, född omkring 1358, död 1416, hertiginna av Sachsen-Lauenburg. Dotter till hertig Magnus II av Braunschweig-Lüneburg (stupad 1373) och Katharina av Anhalt-Bernburg (död 1390).Sophia gifte sig 8 apri
1 CONC l 1373 med hertig Erik IV av Sachsen-Lauenburg (1354-1412). Paret fick följande barn:1. Erik V av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död 1436), hertig av Sachsen-Lauenburg 2. Johan IV av Sachsen-Lauenburg (dödad 1414), hertig av Sachsen-Lauenburg
1 CONC  3. Albrecht av Sachsen-Lauenburg (dödad 1421), kanik i Hildesheim 4. Magnus av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död 1452), biskop av Hildesheim och Kammin 5. Bernhard II av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död 1463), hertig av Sachsen-Lauenburg 6. Otto av S
1 CONC achsen-Lauenburg (död före 1431) 7. Agnes av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död före 1415), gift med greve Albrekt II av Holstein (död 1403) 8. Agnes av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död cirka 1435), gift med hertig Wartislav VIII av Pommern (1373-1415)
1 CONC  9. Katharina av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död tidigast 1448), gift 1. med furst Johann VII av Mecklenburg-Werle (död 1414), gift 2. med hertig Johan IV av Mecklenburg (död 1422) 10. Sophie av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död 1462), gift med herti
1 CONC g Wartislav IX av Pommern (död 1457) 
0 @N6000000002188383213@ NOTE Eric XII of Sweden
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eric XII Magnusson (1339 – June 21, 1359) was rival King of Sweden to his father Magnus II from 1356 to his death in 1359. Married to Beatrix of Bavaria, daughter of Louis IV of Bavaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1343 Eric and his brother Haakon were elected heirs of Sweden respectively Norway. That Haakon got the Norwegian throne in 1355 (causing the union between Norway and Sweden to split) while Eric didn't get any position in the Sw
1 CONC edish council might have affected his choice to lead a rebellion against his father in 1355. In 1357 the rebellion had forced Magnus to share Sweden with his son Eric who got to rule most of Southern Sweden and Finland. Sweden was
1 CONC  reunited again in 1359 when father and son became reconciled, and co-ruled Sweden until the Eric's death a few months later. While dying Eric accused his mother Blanche of Namur for poisoning him. Quite soon after the death his w
1 CONC ife Beatrix died too. It is generally believed that they were died of plague.
1 CONT Erik Magnusson
1 CONT House of Bjelbo
1 CONT Born: 1339 Died: 1359
