0 @N6000000002187825484@ NOTE William II, Duke of Bavaria-Straubing
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke William II of Bavaria-Straubing was also count William VI of Holland, count William IV of Hainaut and count William V of Zeeland. He ruled from 1404 to his death in 1417. William was a son of Albert I and married to Margaret,
1 CONC  sister of John, Duke of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT William and John defeated the bishop of Liege at the battle of Othée in 1408 meaning that he was no longer, as count of Hainault, obliged to pay homage to the bishop. William's reign was marked by internal strife within the county
1 CONC  of Holland. In particular, Lord John of Arkel supported William's enemies in Holland. Arkel became a part of Holland in 1412 at which point John accepted his defeat at the hands of William.
1 CONT
1 CONT William claimed Friesland as the count of Holland. Duke William I of Bavaria-Straubing had previously sent five expeditions to conquer Friesland. Only Stavoren was captured in 1398. William II also sent expeditions to the region b
1 CONC ut Stavoren was regained by the Frisians in 1414. Prior to his death, William ensured his nobles swore allegiance to his only daughter, Jacqueline.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, on William's death in 1417, a war of succession broke out between John III and William's daughter Jacqueline of Hainaut. John III was a younger brother of William, who had become Bishop of Liège.
1 CONT
1 CONT This would be the last episode of the Hook and Cod wars and finally place Holland and Hainaut into Burgundian hands. Bavaria-Straubing was divided between the dukes of Bavaria, the major portion went to Bavaria-Munich in 1429.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Counts of Hainaut family tree 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Albert I and Albert II Duke of Bavaria-Straubing
1 CONT 1404–1417 Succeeded by:
1 CONT John III 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Albert I Count of Hainaut, Holland, and Zeeland
1 CONT 1404–1417 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Jacqueline 
0 @N6000000002187825474@ NOTE Albert II, Duke of Bavaria-Straubing
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Albert II or Albrecht ( born 1368, – January 21, 1397, Kelheim) was a feudal co-regent of his father Albert I, Duke of Bavaria in the counties of Holland, Hainaut, and Zeeland in the Low Countries. Additionally he administrat
1 CONC ed from 1389 until his death in 1397 the Bavarian province of Straubing in the name of his father, it being his Bavarian ducal line's appanage and seat.
0 @N6000000002322305753@ NOTE Crowned King of Bohemia in 1376 and King of the Romans in 1376. His reign,
1 CONT weak and tempestuous, though eventful, was continually plagued by wars and
1 CONT princely rivalries that he was unable to control, plunging his territories
1 CONT into a state of virtual anarchy until he was stripped of his powers
1 CONT altogether by a rebellious nobility, naming Rupert (Ruprecht) III, elector
1 CONT Palatine, as King of the Romans (future Holy Roman Emperor), and deposing
1 CONT Wenceslas in 1400, leaving his title of King of Bohemia intact for life.
