0 @N6000000002187780904@ NOTE James Stewart, Duke of Ross
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT James Stewart, Duke of Ross (March 1476 - January 1504) was the son of King James III of Scotland and Margaret of Denmark. He was made Marquess of Ormonde at his baptism, Earl of Ross in 1481, and Duke of Ross in 1488.
1 CONT
1 CONT Stewart became Archbishop of St. Andrews in 1497 and Lord Chancellor of Scotland in 1502.
1 CONT
1 CONT Stewart had two brothers, King James IV of Scotland and John Stewart, Earl of Mar. It seems surprising that there were two brothers both called James; it has been suggested that at the time the younger was born the older was serio
1 CONC usly ill and seemed unlikely to survive, but it is unclear whether there is any evidence for this hypothesis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Religious Posts 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William Scheves Archbishop of St. Andrews
1 CONT 1497–1504 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alexander Stewart 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT George Crichton Commendator of Dunfermline
1 CONT 1500–1504 Succeeded by:
1 CONT James Beaton *
1 CONT *His immediate successor may have been Gilbert Strachan. 
1 CONT Political offices 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT George Gordon, 2nd Earl of Huntly Lord Chancellor of Scotland
1 CONT 1502–1504 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alexander Stewart 
1 CONT Peerage of Scotland 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Duke of Ross
1 CONT 1481–1504 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Extinct 
1 CONT Marquess of Ormonde
1 CONT 1488–1504 
1 CONT Earl of Ross
1 CONT 1481–1504 
0 @N6000000002187780831@ NOTE James IV of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT James IV (March 17, 1473 – September 9, 1513) was king of Scotland from 1488 to 1513.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of King James III and Margaret of Denmark, he was probably born in Stirling Castle. When his father was killed at the Battle of Sauchieburn on June 11, 1488 (or possibly assassinated a few hours later) the fifteen-year-old
1 CONC  James took the throne and was crowned at Scone, Perthshire on June 24. The rebels who had gathered at Sauchieburn had done so with James supposedly as their figurehead. When James realised the indirect role which he had played in
1 CONC  the death of his father, he decided to do penance for his sin. From that date on he wore a heavy iron chain round his waist next to the skin as a constant reminder.
1 CONT
1 CONT James IV quickly proved to be an effective ruler. He defeated another rebellion in 1489, took a direct interest in the administration of justice and finally brought the Lord of the Isles under control in 1493. James was well educa
1 CONC ted and it was claimed that he was fluent in Lowland Scots, English, Scottish Gaelic, Latin, French, German, Italian, Flemish, Spanish and Danish.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a true Renaissance prince with an interest in practical and scientific matters. James granted the Edinburgh College of Surgeons a royal charter in 1506, turned Edinburgh Castle into one of Britain's foremost gun foundries a
1 CONC nd welcomed the establishment of Scotland's first printing press in 1507.
1 CONT
1 CONT James also loved ships and saw the importance in Scotland having a large navy. He acquired thirty-eight ships for the Royal Scottish Navy and founded two new dockyards. His finest creation was the carrack Great Michael. Launched i
1 CONC n 1511 she weighed 1,000 tons, was 240 feet in length and was then the largest ship in Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT For a time he supported the pretender to the English throne Perkin Warbeck and carried out a brief invasion of England on his behalf. Having fought off the aggression of King Henry VII of England, James recognized that peace betwe
1 CONC en Scotland and England was in the interest of both countries, and so attempted to maintain peace with his neighbour by agreeing a treaty of "perpetual peace" in 1502 and marrying Henry's daughter Margaret Tudor, on August 8, 1503
1 CONC , at Holyrood Abbey, Edinburgh. The couple's first three children all died in infancy. Their son James V survived, and he also had a posthumous son, Alexander, who died in infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT When war broke out between England and France as a result of the Italian Wars, James found himself in a difficult position. The new king of England, Henry VIII, attempted to invade France in 1513, and James reacted by declaring wa
1 CONC r on England. Hoping to take advantage of Henry's absence, he led an invading army southward, only to be killed, with many of his nobles and common soldiers, at the disastrous Battle of Flodden Field on September 9, ending Scotlan
1 CONC d's involvement in the War of the League of Cambrai. A body thought to be his was recovered from the battlefield and taken to London for burial. As he was excommunicated, the embalmed body lay unburied for many years in the monast
1 CONC ery of Sheen in Surrey, and was lost after the Reformation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rumors persisted that he had survived and had gone into exile, but there is no evidence to support them.
1 CONT
1 CONT James also had seven illegitimate children by four different mistresses: three by Janet Kennedy, two by Marion Boyd, and one each by Margaret Drummond and Isabel Buchan. Two by Janet Kennedy died in infancy, three daughters and tw
1 CONC o sons reached adulthood. Alexander Stewart, his son by Marion Boyd, whom James had made Archbishop of St. Andrews, died at the Battle of Flodden. His other illegitimate son was James Stewart, Earl of Moray.
1 CONT
1 CONT James IV is also significant in Scottish history as the last King of Scots to have been fluent in Scottish Gaelic.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT James III King of Scots Succeeded by:
1 CONT James V 
0 @N6000000002134213235@ NOTE JAMES IV, KING OF SCOTS (1473-1513)
1 CONT The misfortune that brought James IV to the throne caused him to wear an iron chain around his body as a penance. Any doubts about his immaturity and fitness to govern soon disappeared, however, and he proved himself a strong lead
1 CONC er, defeating those who opposed him. No doubt because of James's personality and interest in the arts, the great European scholar Erasmus (for a time tutor to one of the king's illegitimate sons) praised the king's intellect and k
1 CONC nowledge. An Act of Parliament made it compulsory for men of substance to have their sons educated in arts and law and in 1508 the printing press was introduced into Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Though much of the Scottish nobility, especially in the Lowlands was switching to English, James learned to speak Gaelic, the language of the Highlands and Western Isles. His country enjoyed enormous prestige, holding the balance 
1 CONC of power between ever-warring England and France. He believed that Scotland could lead the way in the glorious cause of freeing Constantinople from Turkish rule. Accordingly, he had the mighty warship the Michael constructed, thus
1 CONC  setting in motion the beginnings of a Scottish shipbuilding industry that in later times would become the envy of the world.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1501, James chose Margaret Tudor, daughter of Henry VII, as his bride following an agreement between the two kings that promised a treaty of perpetual peace and was blessed by the Pope. His continued efforts as peacemaker gave 
1 CONC him the title Rex Pacificator. All seemed well in Scotland. Then the dam burst.
1 CONT
1 CONT England was ruled by the ambitious and ruthless Henry VIII (James's father-in-law), who had entered into an alliance against France with the Pope, the King of Spain and the Doge of Venice. Convinced that the survival of France was
1 CONC  essential to the stability of the whole continent, James did not join this alliance. Instead, he renewed the "Auld Alliance" with France that had begun in 1422 under the regency of Albany. When France appealed to its new Scottish
1 CONC  partner for help, James foolishly sent an ultimatum to the English king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's response was typical. He declared himself "the verie owner of Scotland" a land he considered to be held by the Scottish king in homage. James marched south at the head of a large army to teach him a lesson. The result was 
1 CONC Flodden, one of the most disastrous battles in Scottish history. James was killed, along with his natural son Alexander, thousands of the best and brightest of Scotland's young men, many of its bravest Highland chiefs, great churc
1 CONC h leaders and much of its nobility, not to mention the countless dead among the poor peasant ranks (that made up most of the armies of the period) whose losses were felt throughout the land on farm and croft. The outcome of the ba
1 CONC ttle was felt for many years to come and resentments (and the desire for revenge) lasted for centuries.
0 @N6000000002176666455@ NOTE Reigned as King of Scotland from 1488-1513.
