0 @N6000000002188342334@ NOTE Ferdinand II of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Ferdinand II
1 CONT King of Aragon, King of Castile and Léon
1 CONT Reign 1479-1516
1 CONT Born 10 March 1452(1452-03-10)
1 CONT Died January 23, 1516 (aged 63)
1 CONT Spain
1 CONT Predecessor John II (Aragon)
1 CONT Louis XII (Naples)
1 CONT Successor Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT Consort i) Isabella of Castile
1 CONT ii) Germaine of Foix
1 CONT Issue Joanna of Castile
1 CONT Isabella of Asturias
1 CONT Juan, Prince of Asturias
1 CONT Maria of Aragon
1 CONT Catherine of Aragon
1 CONT Royal House House of Trastamara
1 CONT Father John II
1 CONT Mother Juana Enriquez
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand II of Aragon the Catholic (Spanish: Fernando II de Aragón y V de Castilla "el Católico", Catalan: Ferran II d'Aragó "el Catòlic", Aragonese: Ferrando II d'Aragón "lo Catolico"; March 10, 1452 – January 23, 1516) was king
1 CONC  of Aragon (1479–1516), Sicily (1468–1516), Naples (1504–1516), Valencia, Sardinia and Navarre, Count of Barcelona, jure uxoris King of Castile (1474-1504) and regent and true ruler of this country from 1508 to his death, in the n
1 CONC ame of his mentally challenged daughter Joanna the Mad.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Biography
1 CONT           o 1.1 Acquiring titles
1 CONT           o 1.2 After Isabella
1 CONT           o 1.3 Legacy
1 CONT     * 2 Ancestry
1 CONT     * 3 See also
1 CONT     * 4 References
1 CONT     * 5 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Acquiring titles
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand was the son of the (whose family was a cadet branch of the House of ) by his 2nd wife, the Castilian noblewoman Juana Enriquez. He married Infanta Isabella, the half-sister and heiress of Henry IV of Castile, on October 
1 CONC 19, 1469 in Valladolid and became jure uxoris King of Castile when Isabella succeeded her brother as Queen of Castile in 1474. Isabel also belonged to the royal House of Trastámara. The two young monarchs were initially obliged to
1 CONC  fight a civil war against Joan, princess of Castile (also known as Juana la Beltraneja), the purported daughter of Henry IV, but were ultimately unsuccessful. When Ferdinand succeeded his father as King of Aragon in 1479, the Cro
1 CONC wn of Castile and the various territories of the Crown of Aragon were united in a personal union creating for the first time since the 8th century a single political unit which might be called Spain, although the various territori
1 CONC es were not properly administered as a single unit until the 18th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first decades of Ferdinand and Isabella's joint rule were taken up with the conquest of the Kingdom of Granada, the last Muslim enclave in the Iberian peninsula, which was completed by 1492. In that same year, the Alhambra Dec
1 CONC ree was issued, expelling the Jews from both Castile and Aragon, and Christopher Columbus was sent by the couple on his infamously accidental expedition to the new world. By the Treaty of Tordesillas of 1494, the extra-European wo
1 CONC rld was split between the crowns of Portugal and Castile by a north-south line through the Atlantic Ocean.
1 CONT Wedding portrait of King Ferdinand II of Aragón and Queen Isabella of Castile.
1 CONT Wedding portrait of King Ferdinand II of Aragón and Queen Isabella of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT The latter part of Ferdinand's life was largely taken up with disputes over control of Italy with successive Kings of France, the so-called Italian Wars. In 1494, Charles VIII of France invaded Italy and expelled Alfonso II (who w
1 CONC as Ferdinand's first cousin once removed and stepson of Ferdinand's sister) from the throne of Naples. Ferdinand allied with various Italian princes and with Emperor Maximilian I, to expel the French by 1496 and install Alfonso's 
1 CONC son, Ferdinand, on the Neapolitan throne. In 1501, following the death of Ferdinand II of Naples and his succession by his uncle Frederick, Ferdinand of Aragon signed an agreement with Charles VIII's successor, Louis XII, who had 
1 CONC just successfully asserted his claims to the Duchy of Milan, to partition Naples between them, with Campania and the Abruzzi, including Naples itself, going to the French and Ferdinand taking Apulia and Calabria. The agreement soo
1 CONC n fell apart, and over the next several years, Ferdinand's great general Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba conquered Naples from the French, having succeeded by 1504. Another less famous "conquest" took place in 1503, when Andreas Pale
1 CONC ologus, de jure Emperor of the Eastern Roman Empire, left Ferdinand and Isabella as heirs to the empire, thus Ferdinand became de jure Roman Emperor..
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] After Isabella
1 CONT Aragonese, Valencian and Sicilian Royalty
1 CONT House of Trastámara
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand I
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Alfonso (future Alfonso V of Aragon, III of Valencia and I of Sicily and Naples)
1 CONT    John (future John II of Aragon, Valencia and Navarre and I of Sicily)
1 CONT    Eleanor, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT Alfonso V (III of Valencia and I of Sicily and Naples)
1 CONT    Ferdinand I of Naples (natural son)
1 CONT John II (I of Sicily and II of Navarre)
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Eleanor, Queen of Navarre
1 CONT    Ferdinand (future Ferdinand II of Aragon, Valencia and Sicily, III of Naples, IV of Castile and V of Leon)
1 CONT    Blanca
1 CONT    Joan, Queen of Naples
1 CONT    Charles IV of Navarre
1 CONT Ferdinand II (III of Naples, IV of Castile and V of Leon)
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Isabella, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT    Joan, Queen of Castile
1 CONT    Juan, Prince of Asturias
1 CONT    Mary, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT    Catherine, Queen of England
1 CONT Grandchildren include
1 CONT    Miguel da Paz, Crown Prince of Portugal and Spain
1 CONT    Charles (future Charles I of Spain and V of the Holy Roman Empire)
1 CONT
1 CONT After Isabella's death, her kingdom went to their daughter Joanna. Ferdinand served as the latter's regent during her absence in the Netherlands, ruled by her husband Archduke Philip. Ferdinand attempted to retain the regency perm
1 CONC anently, but was rebuffed by the Castilian nobility and replaced with Joanna's husband, who became Philip I of Castile. After Philip's death in 1506, with Joanna mentally unstable, and her and Philip's son Charles of Ghent was onl
1 CONC y six years old, Ferdinand resumed the regency, ruling through Francisco Cardinal Jimenez de Cisneros, the Chancellor of the Kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand disagreed with Philip's policies. In 1505, Ferdinand remarried to Germaine of Foix, a granddaughter of his half-sister Queen Leonor of Navarre, in hopes of fathering a new heir and so separating Aragon and Castile (denyi
1 CONC ng Philip the governance of Aragon), and to potentially lay claim to Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand also had children from his mistress, Aldonza Ruiz de Iborre y Alemany of Cervera. He had a son, Alfonso de Aragon (born in 1469), who later became archbishop of Zaragoza, and a daughter Joanna (born in 1471), who married
1 CONC  Bernardino de Valsco, the 1st Duke of Frias.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1508, war resumed in Italy, this time against Venice, which all the other powers on the peninsula, including Louis XII, Ferdinand, Maximilian, and Pope Julius II joined together against as the League of Cambrai. Although the Fr
1 CONC ench were victorious against Venice at the Battle of Agnadello, the League soon fell apart, as both the Pope and Ferdinand became suspicious of French intentions. Instead, the Holy League was formed, in which now all the powers jo
1 CONC ined together against France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In November 1511 Ferdinand and his son-in-law Henry VIII of England signed the Treaty of Westminster, pledging mutual aid between the two against France. Earlier that year, Ferdinand had conquered the southern half of the Kingdom 
1 CONC of Navarre, which was ruled by a French nobleman, and annexed it to Spain. The Holy League was generally successful in Italy, as well, driving the French from Milan, which was restored to its Sforza dukes by the peace treaty in 15
1 CONC 13. The French were successful in reconquering Milan two years later, however.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand died in 1516 in Madrigalejo, Cáceres, Extremadura, Spain.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand and Isabel had together made Spain the most powerful country in Europe. The succession of his grandson Charles, who would inherit not only the Spanish lands of his maternal grandparents, but the Habsburg and Burgundian l
1 CONC ands of his paternal family, would make his heirs the most powerful rulers on the continent. Charles succeeded him in the Aragonese lands, and was also granted the Castilian crown jointly with his insane mother, bringing about at 
1 CONC long last the unification of the Spanish thrones under one head.
1 CONT
