1 CONT Joanna of Aragon (also Juana of Aragon) (1454, Barcelona- 9 January 1517, Naples) was an infanta of the Kingdom of Aragon and second Queen consort of Ferdinand I of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the only daughter of King John II of Aragon and his second wife, a noblewoman, Juana Enríquez.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a younger paternal half-sister of Charles of Viana, Blanca of Navarre and Eleanor of Navarre. She was also a younger sister of Ferdinand II of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT On September 14, 1476, Joanna married her paternal first cousin Ferdinand I of Naples. The bride was twenty-two years old and the groom forty-three.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand I was an illegitimate son of Alfonso V of Aragon and his mistress Giraldona Carlino. Alfonso V and her own father John II of Aragon were brothers, both sons of Ferdinand I of Aragon and Eleanor of Alburquerque.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joanna and Ferdinand had two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Giovanna of Naples (1478 - August 27, 1518). Queen consort to her nephew Ferdinand II of Naples, who was actually her elder.
1 CONT    2. Carlo of Naples (1480-1486).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand I of Naples died on 25 January 1494 and was succeeded by Alfonso II of Naples, his son from a previous marriage to Isabella of Taranto. Joanna survived him by twenty-three years and continued to live in the court of Napl
1 CONC es as a Queen Dowager.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Isabella of Taranto Queen consort of Naples
1 CONT 14 September 1476 - 25 January 1494 Succeeded by
1 CONT Giovanna of Naples
0 @N6000000002322298742@ NOTE He was the 10th king of Portugal, known as the African from his campaigns in
1 CONT Morroco. Having assumed the title of Castile, he was, nevertheless defeated
1 CONT at Toro in 1476. Then, he sailed to France to enlist the aid of Louis XI of
1 CONT France. Refused, he informed his son (later King John II) that he would
1 CONT abdicate and become a hermit in France, but he was persuaded to return to
1 CONT Portugal in November 1477. Then, he signed a treaty renouncing any claim to
1 CONT Castile in 1479, but died before the Cortes could ratify the abdication.
