0 @N6000000003403243679@ NOTE [flemingjohn_descendants10_fromrootsweb_tomblumberg_1752723.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Governess to the child Mary Queen of Scots, she accompanied her to France in 1548 for her betrothal to the Dauphin.  In the train were "four Marys": her daughter, Mary Fleming, and three other girls of the same age all named Mary.
1 CONC  At the French co urt, Lady Janet caught the eye of King Henry II, which aroused the wrath of his mistress, Diane de Poitiers. Lady Janet was sent back to Scotland, but the "four Marys" remained attached to the Queen all her life
0 @N6000000002187780818@ NOTE Lady Janet Stewart
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Lady Janet Stewart (c.1505 – c.1563) was an illegitimate daughter of King James IV of Scotland. Her mother was probably Agnes Stewart, herself an illegitimate daughter of James Stewart, 1st Earl of Buchan, but may have been Isobel
1 CONC  Stewart, the earl's legitimate daughter.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lady Janet married Malcolm Fleming, 3rd Lord Fleming, who was killed at the Battle of Pinkie in 1547. She became a governess to the infant Queen Mary I of Scotland, and her own daughter, Mary Fleming, became a lady-in-waiting. The
1 CONC y accompanied the young queen to France in 1548, and there Lady Janet became one of the mistresses of King Henry II of France and had an illegitimate son by him. After the child's birth, she was sent back to Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her son Henri de Valois (1551- June 1586) was legitimized and became governor of Provence.
0 @N6000000000337133323@ NOTE (Research):Lady Janet Stewart (-1563)  daughter of James IV, King of Scots 1488-1513 and Agnes Stewart  Died before 20 February 1563 Married to Malcolm, Lord Fleming  Affaire with Henri II, King of France 1547-1559  Born 31 March 
1 CONC 1519 St.Germain en Laye  Died 10 July 1559 Paris  Buried St.Denis    Lady Janet Stewart was an illegitimate daughter of King James IV of Scots by Agnes Stewart, a daughter, also illegitimate, of James Stewart, Earl of Buchan. Afte
1 CONC r her birth her mother married the Earl of Bothwell. Lady Janet Stewart was probably only about fifteen when she married Malcolm, Lord Fleming, who was then about twenty-five, and they became the parents of at least six children. 
1 CONC However, when Lord Fleming was in his fifty-third year, he fell in the Battle of Pinkie.  When the infant Mary, Queen of Scots, went to France, the widowed Lady Fleming went with her as governess. The party embarked on 29 July 154
1 CONC 8 and waited until on 7 August the required wind allowed them to sail. During this period of forced waiting Lady Fleming became bored and demanded to be put ashore. However, the captain replied that Lady  Fleming, so far from bein
1 CONC g able to go on land, could go to France and like it or drown on the way. Amongst the other attendants was also Lady Fleming's daughter, Mary, who, as one of the four "Maries", also attended Mary, Queen of Scots.  On arrival in Fr
1 CONC ance, Lady Fleming's "charms" were very much appreciated. According to the Venetian ambassador she was "a very pretty little woman". Antoinette de Guise was very pleased with her granddaughter, Mary, Queen of Scots, but much less 
1 CONC so with the  attendants whom she regarded, with the exception of Lady Fleming, as ill-looking and not even properly washed.  As Mary was to be first and foremost Queen of France, most of her attendants were returned to Scotland. H
1 CONC owever, Lady Fleming was again an exception and remained in France. The reason may well have been that she found favour in the eyes of the French king, Henri II. Soon Lady Fleming scandalised the French with her remark: "I have do
1 CONC ne  all I can, and God be thanked, I am pregnant by the king, for which I count myself both honoured and happy." However, her happiness was short-lived as, after she had given birth to a son, she was sent back to Scotland thanks t
1 CONC o the efforts not only of Henri II's queen,  Catherine de' Medici, but also of his mistress, Diane de Poitiers.  Lady Fleming died in Scotland between 5 October 1560 and 20 February 1563. Source: Leo van de Pas
0 @N6000000006127223173@ NOTE Kristina av Holstein-Gottorp föddes den 12 april 1573 som dotter till hertig Adolf av Holstein-Gottorp och lantgrevinnan Kristina av Hessen. Kristina var före sitt giftermål med Karl IX förlovad med polska kungen Sigismund. De had
1 CONC e "växlat porträtt och han hade gett henne gåvor" men sedan brutit förlovningen. Detta ledde till att hon hyste motvilja mot honom, vilket det sägs att honäven överförde till Karl IX.Den 22 augusti 1592, när hon var nitton år gamm
1 CONC al, gifte hon sig på Nyköping slott med hertig Karl, den blivande kung Karl IX, som då var nästan 42 år gammal. De fick med tiden fyra barn:1) Kristina Vasa (1593-1594) (död 1 år)2) Gustav (II) Adolf Vasa (1594-1632)3) Maria Elisa
1 CONC bet Vasa (1596-1618)4) Karl Filip Vasa (1601-1622)År 1607 kröntes hon i Uppsala domkyrka tillsammans med sin man, till kung och drottning av Sverige. Fyra år senare dog Karl IX (1611). Kristina hade då förmyndarskapet över sonen K
1 CONC arl Filips hertigdömen Södermanland, Närke och Värmland. Hon drevekonomin så skickligt i dessa landskap att hon kunde låna ut stora summor pengar till staten.Det sägs att Kristina var mycket inblandad i barnens äktenskapsplaner, k
1 CONC anske lite väl mycket ibland, kan tyckas i våra dagar. Hon förbjöd till exempel sonen Gustav Adolf att gifta sig med sin kära Ebba Brahe - och hennes dotter Maria Elisabettvingades in i ett giftermål med sin kusin, Johan, som var 
1 CONC son till Johan III. Det äktenskapet blev sedan mycket olyckligt.När hennes son Karl Filip dog 1622, drog hon sig tillbaka och tillbringade de sista åren på Nyköpings slott tillsammans med sin syster, prinsessan Agnes, och Karl Fil
1 CONC ips dotter, Elisabeth Karlsdotter Gyllenhjelm, som han fick i ett hemligtäktenskap med Elisabeth Ribbing. Kristina dog den 8 december 1625 på Nyköpings slott, 52 år och 7 månader gammal. Gravplats: Strängnäs domkyrka  
0 @N6000000006127657450@ NOTE Sophie von Mecklenburg (* 4. September 1557 in Wismar, † 3. Oktober 1631 in Nykøbing), war Tochter von Herzog Ulrich zu Mecklenburg und dessen Frau Elisabeth, geborene Prinzessin von Dänemark.Im Alter von erst 14 Jahren heiratete 
1 CONC sie den 23 Jahre älteren König Friedrich II. von Dänemark; trotzdem war es eine harmonische Ehe. Sie gewann nach dem Tode ihres Gemahls 1588 als Vormund über ihren Sohn Christian IV. in Schleswig-Holsteinpolitischen Einfluss. Hier
1 CONC  kam sie in Konflikt mit der Regierung und wurde nach Nykøbing auf die Insel Falster vertrieben.Sie bewies sich als fähige Verwalterin ihrer Güter in Lolland und Falster. Sophie wurde sehr reich und tätigte Geldgeschäfte. Sie lieh
1 CONC  vielen europäischen Fürsten Geld, darunter auch ihrem eigenen Sohn für die Finanzierung seiner militärischenUnternehmungen.Nachkommen  Sophie heiratete König Friedrich II. von Dänemark.Elisabeth (1573-1625) - verheiratet mit Herz
1 CONC og Heinrich Julius von Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel Anna (1574-1619) - verheiratet mit König Jakob I. von England Christian IV. (1577-1648) Ulrich Johann (1578-1624) Auguste Johann Augusta (1580-1639) - verheiratet mit Johann Adolf v
1 CONC on Schleswig-Holstein-Gottorf Hedwig (1581-1641) - verheiratet mit Kurfürst Christian II. von Sachsen Johann (1583-1602) 
