0 @N6000000001148163330@ NOTE Juan Bautista Rodriguez de Sotolongo was armado caballero [knighted] en la Vega de Granada, on June 22, 1455, by Enrique IV of Castile and León. In 1455, Castile undertook the first of six invasions of Granada, which had been held
1 CONC  by the Moors for eight centuries, where, it would appear, Juan Bautista Rodriguez performed some act of heroism, which earned him his knighthood.
1 CONT
1 CONT Juan Bautista Rodríguez de Sotolongo was the son of Rodrigo Alfonso. Rodríguez means "the son of Rodrigo" so it appears that Juan Bautista was the first in his family to adopt, or be given, a formal "family" name - Rodríguez - in 
1 CONC place of the patronymic system in which, for example, his father, son of Alfonso Nuño, was called Rodrigo, son of Alfonso, which later became Rodrigo Alfonso.
1 CONT
1 CONT We don't know the name of Juan Bautista's mother and it may have been Sotolongo in which case Juan Batista's full name would have been Juan Bautista Rodríguez y Sotolongo. More likely, especially in light of his knighthood, the "d
1 CONC e Sotolongo" may have been nothing more than an embellishment to distinguish him from more ordinary Rodriguez'.
1 CONT
1 CONT In any case, the Rodríguez de Sotolongo name didn't last for long because his son was called Juan Bautista de Sotolongo.
1 CONT
1 CONT The record shows that Juan Bautista Rodriguez de Sotolongo, was named Knight of the Vega of Granada on June 22, 1455, by Enrique IV, King of Castile. Juan Bautista Rodriguez de Sotolongo was the son of Leonor de Soto Duchess of Vi
1 CONC lla-hermosa and Alonso of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alonso of Aragon was the son of King Juan II and V of Castile and brother of King Fernardo the Catholic.
1 CONT
1 CONT The study from the Conde de Jaruco you are referring to, is it the SOTOLONGO surname starting on page 281 I believe of volume II?
1 CONT
1 CONT If that is correct, I have copies of the whole surname. Look at the first page were it talks about Juan Bautista Rodriguez de Sotolongo father and grandfather, and then read the last paragraph at the bottom of the page. What can y
1 CONC ou make of it?
1 CONT
1 CONT It is quite intriguing, maybe he was an illegitimate child. I think because the bottom note was added 300 years after the fact, some descendant was trying to proof his heraldry. Maybe for money or status.
