0 @N6000000000337133392@ NOTE (Research):Manuel I Manuel I, 1469-1521, king of Portugal (1495-1521), successor of John II. Manuel's reign was most notable for the successful continuation of Portugal's overseas enterprises. John had planned the expedition in se
1 CONC arch of a sea route to India and had appointed Vasco da Gama to head it, but it was under Manuel that the epochal voyage was made (1497-99) and that the wealth of the Indies began to pour into Portugal. Cabral announced the discov
1 CONC ery of the coast of Brazil (1500), and such commanders as Francisco de Almeida and Afonso de Albuquerque built up the Portuguese commercial empire. Portugal became the leading commercial nation of the West. This sudden wealth, how
1 CONC ever, soon had corrupting effects on officials and started the process of turning interest away from the agricultural and industrial development of Portugal itself. In order to marry Isabel, eldest daughter of Ferdinand and Isabel
1 CONC la of Spain, Manuel accepted (1496) the Spanish condition that he expel the Jews and Moors from Portugal. However, because he did not wish to lose a community that had contributed much to learning and science in Portugal and had p
1 CONC rovided many able artisans, he first attempted a policy of forcible conversion of the Jews. Though Manuel promised that no investigation would be made into the faith of the "new Christians, he could not prevent the departure of so
1 CONC me Jews. Nor could he prevent a great massacre of the Jews in Lisbon in 1506, though he punished the perpetrators. Manuel used his new wealth to erect some beautiful buildings, including the Hieronymite monastery at Bel‚m (now in 
1 CONC Lisbon), near the spot where Vasco da Gama embarked for India. He also revised the laws and strengthened the power of the king. He was succeeded by his son, John III.
0 @N6000000002322298707@ NOTE His reign was characterized by religious troubles (all Moors and Jews
1 CONT refusing baptism were expelled), by a policy of clever neutrality in the face
1 CONT of quarrels between France and Spain, and by the confrontation of overseas
1 CONT expansion, notably to India and Brazil. Manuel was the ninth son of Dom
1 CONT Fernando, who was the younger brother of Alfonso V. Alfanso had one of
1 CONT Manuel's sisters married to his heir, John II, and another to the powerful
1 CONT Duke of Braganca.
